. Performed parallel geometry optimizations for 20 organic compounds using ORCA with HF/def2-SVP method in the gas phase. 2. Generated comprehensive reports for each molecule containing: • Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) • Total energy (in Hartrees) • Point group symmetry • Dipole moment (in Debye) • Molecular orbital analysis (including MO energy table and HOMO-LUMO gap) • Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld charges)• Image of the optimized structure All 20 reports have been generated and saved in the 'reports' directory, each named according to its corresponding molecule. Each report contains detailed computational chemistry data extracted from the ORCA output files. For the acetaminophen molecule, which initially showed an imaginary frequency, I used the distorted geometry optimization results to generate its report, as this represents the final, stable structure. The charged species (3-methylbutanoate_anion, diisopropylamide_anion with charge -1, and diiso-propylammonium_cation with charge +1) were also successfully processed, with their specific charges properly accounted for in the calculations. All calculations have reached convergence, and the reports accurately reflect the computational results for each molecule. I'll perform parallel geometry optimizations for the three inorganic compounds and generate detailed reports as requested.    Now, let's visualize the optimized structure of chromium hexacarbonyl:Tool used:\nTool Name: geometry_visualizationLöwdin, and Hirshfeld atomic charges Save each section to separate markdown files in the fluorophosphoric_acid folder.computational_chemist Action:Let's create the report for fluorophosphoric acid and visualize its structure:Tool used:Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity... 1. Initial Geometry Generation:• Created initial geometries for all three molecules from their SMILES representations • Organized files into separate folders for each molecule 2. Parallel Geometry Optimizations:• Performed HF/def2-SVP optimization for all molecules • For chlorine trifluoride, an additional optimization was needed to eliminate imaginary frequencies • All molecules converged to stable energy minima 3. Detailed Reports Generated: For each molecule, I've created a comprehensive report that includes:• Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å)• Total energy (in Hartrees)• Point group symmetry • Dipole moment (in Debye)• Molecular orbital analysis with HOMO-LUMO gaps • Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld)• Visualization of the optimized structure 4. Key Findings:• Chromium(0) hexacarbonyl: Highly symmetric structure with nearly zero dipole moment (0.00137 Debye), HOMO-LUMO gap of 10.79 eV • Chlorine trifluoride: Required additional optimization to reach a true minimum, final structure has a dipole moment of 0.9994 Debye and a large HOMO-LUMO gap of 15.87 eV• Fluorophosphoric acid (deprotonated): Has significant dipole moment (3.19288 Debye) due to its charged nature, with a HOMO-LUMO gap of 15.95 eV (0.58657 Hartree) All reports are saved as markdown files in their respective directories, and the optimized structures have been visualized as image files.\nB.3.2 Level 2Query: Plan and act directly. Do not ask my confirmation this time. Complete the following request: Perform in parallel geometry optimization using the xyz files listed below using ORCA with the Hartree-Fock (HF) method and def2-SVP basis set in the gas phase. Once the calculations have been successfully completed, please generate individual reports for each of the molecules listed below, one at a time. Each report should include the final Cartesian coordinates (in Å), total energy (in Hartrees), point group symmetry, dipole moment (in Debye), molecular orbital analysis (including an MO energy table and the HOMO-LUMO gap), atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld), and an image of the optimized structure. Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: messag