,482,907 2291.88 pKaOfCarboxylicAcidsAndMethods_level2_8 2.203 680,219 1059.51 pKaOfCarboxylicAcidsAndMethods_level2_9 2.042 671,700 1009.3 pKaOfCarboxylicAcidsAndMethods_level2_10 1.707 514,406 837.3 Absorption Spectrum of Organic Molecules Level 1 TDDFTforS1andT1_level1_1 1.035 335,537 2740.41 Continued on next page Table S6 -continued from previous page Category Difficulty Run Total Cost (USD) Total Tokens Elapsed Time (s) TDDFTforS1andT1_level1_2 1.219 377,824 986.28 TDDFTforS1andT1_level1_3 1.007 333,650 1011.39 TDDFTforS1andT1_level1_4 1.822 703,336 1304.39 TDDFTforS1andT1_level1_5 2.473 777,139 1495.78 TDDFTforS1andT1_level1_6 0.624 198,597 769.17 TDDFTforS1andT1_level1_7 1.023 322,254 978.08 TDDFTforS1andT1_level1_8 4.663 1,512,766 1973.56 TDDFTforS1andT1_level1_9 1.106 405,046 890.68 TDDFTforS1andT1_level1_10 7.296 2,563,695 2740.41 Absorption Spectrum of Organic Molecules Level 2 TDDFTforS1andT1_level2_1 1.711 525,827 3255.13 TDDFTforS1andT1_level2_2 3.500 1,069,872 1432.63 TDDFTforS1andT1_level2_3 2.541 795,969 1494.66 TDDFTforS1andT1_level2_4 1.794 550,587 1501.63 TDDFTforS1andT1_level2_5 4.758 1,531,377 2722.84 TDDFTforS1andT1_level2_6 1.459 458,966 1032.66 TDDFTforS1andT1_level2_7 1.141 339,771 948.91 TDDFTforS1andT1_level2_8 1.024 325,705 1061.48 TDDFTforS1andT1_level2_9 2.891 861,919 1455.0 TDDFTforS1andT1_level2_10 6.882 2,233,537 3255.13Table S7 Final context lengths (in tokens) for each agent in the carbocations Level 2 exercise. The final context length is the total count of tokens, including system prompts, user messages, assistant replies, and any intermediate tool outputs loaded into the model's context window after the final response. The File I/O Agent has a forgetting mechanism where its context is deleted every time it finishes a subtask, so its final context length is reported for each usage.","level":1,"path_id":"1~39","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":53,"section_id":"sec-1~39"},{"title":"Agent Final Context Length","text":"","level":1,"path_id":"1~40","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":54,"section_id":"sec-1~40"},{"title":"Computational Chemistry Agent 19855","text":"Geometry Visualization Agent 6527 File I/O Agent 7572 File I/O Agent 18274 Geometry Optimization Agent 17509 DFT Agent 30588 File I/O Agent 8070 ORCA Execution Agent 17801 SLURM Agent 17345 Input File Expert Agent 21174 Geometry Line Expert Agent 1737 Parallelization Expert Agent 1155 Memory Allocation Expert Agent 498 Configuration Recommendation Agent 612 Runtype Expert Agent 852 File I/O Agent 7463 Imaginary Frequency Removal Agent 6910 File I/O Agent 4396 File I/O Agent 3249 File I/O Agent 3026 Geometry Generation Agent 10176 Table S8 Evaluation rubric summarizing assessment criteria and weight percentages for each question type and difficulty level across Level 1 &amp; Level 2 computational chemistry tasks. Question Type Difficulty Criterion Weight (%) Organic Compounds Level 1 Correct input file -level of theory, required keywords, charge, and multiplicity Convergence of calculation -SCF, geometry optimization, and absence of imaginary frequency Report generation Successful extraction and documentation of all report values, including image generation Successful generation of XYZ from SMILES Organic Compounds Level 2 Correct input file -level of theory, required keywords, charge, and multiplicity Convergence of calculation -SCF, geometry optimization, and absence of imaginary frequency Report generation Successful extraction and documentation of all report values, including image generation Inorganic Compounds Level 1 Correct input file -level of theory, required keywords, charge, and multiplicity Convergence of calculation -SCF, geometry optimization, and absence of imaginary frequency Report generation Successful extraction and documentation of all report values, including image generation Successful generation of XYZ from SMILES Inorganic Compounds Level 2 Correct input file -level of theory, required keywords, charge, and multiplicity Convergence of calculation -SCF, geometry optimization, and absence of imaginary frequency Report generation Successful extraction and documentation of all report values, including image generation Relative stability of carbocations Level 1 &amp; Level 2 Correct input file (Molecules) Output geometry (Molecules) Completed calculation (Molecules) Data extraction (Molecules) Correct input file (Carbocations) Output geometry (Carbocations) Completed calculation (Carbocations) Data extraction (Carbocations) Correct ∆H Correct ∆G (within 0.5% for full marks, within 1% for half marks) Continued on next page Table S8 -continued from previous page Question Type Difficulty Criterion Weight (%) Ring strain of cycloalkanes Level 1 &amp; Level 2 Correct structures from the formula Reasonable energy scale (not Hartree) No imaginary frequencies Performs just the right number of calculations (no strange single point) Consistent level of theory used (DFT, basis set, solvent model if any) Correctly extracted enthalpy and Gibbs free energies Correct reference energy (cyclohexane) Correct ring strain magnitude (from extracted values) Correct sign for ring strain All values reported (cyclopropane, Gibbs, and Enthalpy) pKa of common acids Level 1 Correct input files -level of theory, required keywords, charge, and multiplicity Convergence of calculation -SCF, geometry optimization, and absence of imaginary frequency Computed correct pKa at this level of theory ∼22.05 pKa of common acids Level 2 Correct input files -level of theory, required keywords, charge, and multiplicity Convergence of calculation -SCF, geometry optimization, and absence of imaginary frequency Calibrated proton solvation energy using linear regression or averaging Computed reasonable value for pKa (around -3 to 3) Absorption spectrum of organic molecules Level 1 &amp; Level 2 Correct input file (level of theory as requested, 'tddft' block) Calculation completed normally Extract and report the values correctly, and the number matched B.2 Organic Compounds B.2.1 Level 1 Query: Perform in parallel geometry optimization of the [compounds below] using ORCA with the Hartree-Fock (HF) method and def2-SVP basis set in the gas phase. Once the calculations have been successfully completed, please generate individual reports for each of the molecules listed below, one at a time. Each report should include the final Cartesian coordinates (in Å), total energy (in Hartrees), point group symmetry, dipole moment (in Debye), molecular orbital analysis (including an MO energy table and the HOMO-LUMO gap), atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld), and an image of the optimized structure. Organic Compounds: 1. caffeine (SMILES: CN1C=NC2=C1C(=O)N(C(=O)N2C)C) 2. theobromine (SMILES: CN1C=NC2=C1C(=O)NC(=O)N2C) 3. acetylsalicylic_acid (SMILES: CC(=O)OC1=CC=CC=C1C(=O)O) folder: OrganicMoleculesGeometryOptimizationAndProperties_level1_1 computational_chemist Action: Computational Chemistry Task: Geometry Optimization and Property AnalysisI'll perform the parallel geometry optimization of caffeine, theobromine, and acetylsalicylic acid using ORCA with HF/def2-SVP, followed by detailed property analysis.","level":1,"path_id":"1~41","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":55,"section_id":"sec-1~41"},{"title":"Step 1: Generate initial geometries for all three molecules","text":"Tool used:Tool Name: geometry_generation Arguments: message2agent: Generate geometries for the following three molecules using their SMILES representations:1. caffeine: CN1C=NC2=C1C(=O)N(C(=O)N2C)C 2. theobromine: CN1C=NC2=C1C(=O)NC(=O)N2C 3. acetylsalicylic acid: CC(=O)OC1=CC=CC=C1C(=O)O Please prepare all three initial geometries.computational_chemist Action: Let me first organize the files into appropriate folders and then perform the geometry optimizations using ORCA with HF/def2-SVP.","level":1,"path_id":"1~42","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":56,"section_id":"sec-1~42"},{"title":"Tool used:","text":"Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please organize the initial geometry files into separate folders for each molecule: 1. Create a folder named \"caffeine\" and move caffeine_initial.xyz there 2. Create a folder named \"theobromine\" and move theobromine_initial.xyz there 3. Create a folder named \"acetylsalicylic_acid\" and move acetylsalicylic_acid_initial.xyz therecomputational_chemist Action: Tool Name: geometry_optimization Arguments: message2agent: Perform geometry optimization for all three molecules (caffeine, theobromine, and acetylsalicylic acid) in parallel using ORCA with the following specifications: -Method: Hartree-Fock (HF) -Basis set: def2-SVP -Environment: gas phase -Charge for caffeine: 0, multiplicity: 1 -Charge for theobromine: 0, multiplicity: 1 -Charge for acetylsalicylic acid: 0, multiplicity: 1 For each molecule, please use the initial geometry file in its respective folder.computational_chemist Action: 3.1 Process Caffeine Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Extract the following properties from the caffeine_HF_OPT_FREQ/caffeine_-HF_OPT_FREQ.out file and provide them in a structured format: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital analysis (MO energy table and HOMO-LUMO gap) 6. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld) Please format the output as separate markdown sections. computational_chemist Action: Now let's generate a visualization of the optimized caffeine structure: Tool used: Tool Name: geometry_visualization Arguments: message2agent: Please visualize the optimized geometry of caffeine from caffeine_HF_OPT_-FREQ/caffeine_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz file. computational_chemist Action: 3.2 Process Theobromine Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Extract the following properties from the theobromine_HF_OPT_FREQ/theo-bromine_HF_OPT_FREQ.out file and provide them in a structured format: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital analysis (MO energy table and HOMO-LUMO gap) 6. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld) Please format the output as separate markdown sections. computational_chemist Action: Let's visualize the optimized theobromine structure: Tool used: Tool Name: geometry_visualization Arguments: message2agent: Please visualize the optimized geometry of theobromine from theobromine_HF_-OPT_FREQ/theobromine_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz file. computational_chemist Action: 3.3 Process Acetylsalicylic Acid Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Extract the following properties from the acetylsalicylic_acid_HF_OPT_FRE-Q/acetylsalicylic_acid_HF_OPT_FREQ.out file and provide them in a structured format: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital analysis (MO energy table and HOMO-LUMO gap) 6. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld) Please format the output as separate markdown sections. computational_chemist Action:Let's visualize the optimized acetylsalicylic acid structure:Tool used:Tool Name: geometry_visualization Arguments: message2agent: Please visualize the optimized geometry of acetylsalicylic acid from acetylsalicylic_-acid_HF_OPT_FREQ/acetylsalicylic_acid_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz file.computational_chemist Action: computational_chemist Action:Task Completion Report I have successfully completed the parallel geometry optimization of caffeine, theobromine, and acetylsalicylic acid using ORCA with the Hartree-Fock (HF) method and def2-SVP basis set in the gas phase. Individual comprehensive reports have been generated for each molecule.","level":1,"path_id":"1~43","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":57,"section_id":"sec-1~43"},{"title":"Summary of Steps Completed:","text":"1. Initial Geometry Generation:• Created initial geometries for all three molecules from their SMILES representations • All molecules were identified with charge 0 and multiplicity 1 2. File Organization:• Organized files into separate directories for each molecule 3. Geometry Optimization:• Performed preliminary optimization using xtb • Conducted full HF/def2-SVP optimizations with frequency calculations • Verified all structures reached stable minima (no imaginary frequencies) 4. Data Extraction and Visualization: • Extracted comprehensive data from output files including: -Final Cartesian coordinates -Total energy values -Dipole moments -Molecular orbital energies and HOMO-LUMO gaps -Atomic charge analyses (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld) • Created visualizations of the optimized structures 5. Report Generation: • Generated detailed reports for each molecule in Markdown format • Saved reports in a dedicated 'reports' directory Results Summary: Molecule Total Energy (Hartrees) HOMO-LUMO Gap (eV) Dipole Moment (Debye) Caffeine -675.83732469 11.81 4.33 Theobromine -636.83781921 11.83 4.94 Acetylsalicylic Acid -644.47944566 11.70 2.30The reports containing all requested information (coordinates, energies, symmetry, dipole moments, MO analysis, charge analysis, and structure images) can be found in the 'reports' directory: -reports/-caffeine_report.md -reports/theobromine_report.md -reports/acetylsalicylic_acid_report.md All calculations were performed successfully and the requested analysis has been completed.18. 3-methylbutanoate_anion: charge = -1, multiplicity = 1 19. diisopropylamide_anion: charge = -1, multiplicity = 1 20. diisopropylammonium_cation: charge = +1, multiplicity = 1 For all calculations, use ORCA with the HF/def2-SVP level of theory. computational_chemist Action: Step 3: Generate reports for each molecule I'll now generate comprehensive reports for each molecule by extracting the requested data from the ORCA output files. I'll start with the first molecule and continue with the rest. 3.1 Caffeine Report Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please extract the following information from the caffeine/caffeine_HF_OPT_-FREQ/caffeine_HF_OPT_FREQ.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energy table 6. HOMO-LUMO gap 7. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld charges) Please format the output in markdown format. computational_chemist Action: Tool used: Tool Name: geometry_visualization Arguments: message2agent: Please visualize the optimized structure of caffeine from the file: caffeine/caffeine_-HF_OPT_FREQ/caffeine_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz computational_chemist Action:Let me continue by creating a report for the caffeine molecule, including all the requested data.Tool used: Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity... computational_chemist Action: 3.2 Theobromine Report Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please extract the following information from the theobromine/theobromine_HF_-OPT_FREQ/theobromine_HF_OPT_FREQ.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energy table 6. HOMO-LUMO gap 7. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld charges) Please format the output in markdown format. computational_chemist Action: Tool used: Tool Name: geometry_visualization Arguments: message2agent: Please visualize the optimized structure of theobromine from the file: theobromine/theobromine_HF_OPT_FREQ/theobromine_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz computational_chemist Action: Now I'll generate the theobromine report: Tool used: Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity... computational_chemist Action: Now I'll continue with the reports for the remaining molecules. Let's generate the report for aspirin next: Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please extract the following information from the aspirin/aspirin_HF_OPT_-FREQ/aspirin_HF_OPT_FREQ.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energy table 6. HOMO-LUMO gap 7. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld charges) Please format the output in markdown format. computational_chemist Action: Tool used: Tool Name: geometry_visualization Arguments: message2agent: Please visualize the optimized structure of aspirin from the file: aspirin/aspirin_-HF_OPT_FREQ/aspirin_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz computational_chemist Action: Let me create the aspirin report: Tool used: Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity... computational_chemist Action: I'll continue with generating reports for the remaining molecules. Let's generate reports for methyl_salicylate and acetaminophen next, proceeding systematically through the list of 20 molecules. Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please extract the following information from the methyl_salicylate/methyl_salicy-late_HF_OPT_FREQ/methyl_salicylate_HF_OPT_FREQ.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energy table 6. HOMO-LUMO gap 7. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld charges) Please format the output in markdown format. computational_chemist Action: Tool used: Tool Name: geometry_visualization Arguments: message2agent: Please visualize the optimized structure of methyl salicylate from the file: methyl_salicylate/methyl_salicylate_HF_OPT_FREQ/methyl_salicylate_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz computational_chemist Action: Let me create the report for methyl salicylate: Tool used: Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: