Criteria Tight\" 3. Second submission attempt:• Jobs failed again with error: \"Unknown identifier in SCF block line 13: Last token: CON-VCRITERIA\" • Modified the input files by removing the problematic convergence criteria settings entirely 4. Final successful submission:• All five jobs completed successfully with \"ORCA TERMINATED NORMALLY\" status • Each job directory now contains the expected output files (.out, .xyz, _trj.xyz) The following ORCA geometry optimization and frequency calculations for the Ce(III) complexes are now complete: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ 2. tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ 3. tricapped_-trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ 5. capped_-square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ All output files are available in their respective job directories. Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please check the output files for all five calculations to determine if they converged successfully. For each calculation, check the corresponding .out file in the respective folder and look for \"ORCA TERMINATED NORMALLY\" message and verify that both the geometry optimization and frequency calculations completed successfully. Please report if there are any errors or warnings that need attention.interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used:Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.out query_keys: ['convergence_information'] interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_-OPT_FREQ.out query_keys: ['convergence_information'] Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ.out query_keys: ['convergence_information'] Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ/tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ.out query_keys: ['convergence_information'] Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ/tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_-FREQ.out query_keys: ['convergence_information'] interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.out query_keys: ['convergence_information', 'SCF HESSIAN'] Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_-OPT_FREQ.out query_keys: ['convergence_information', 'SCF HESSIAN'] Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ.out query_keys: ['convergence_information', 'SCF HESSIAN'] Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ/tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ.out query_keys: ['convergence_information', 'SCF HESSIAN'] Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ/tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_-FREQ.out query_keys: ['convergence_information', 'SCF HESSIAN'] interact_with_os_and_files Action:All five calculations were checked for convergence and completion of both geometry optimization and frequency (Hessian) calculations. Here are the results: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ:• SCF converged after 6 cycles.• Geometry optimization converged successfully.• Frequency (Hessian) calculation completed successfully.• Note: \"Only C1 symmetry has been detected, increase convergence thresholds\" (not a critical error, just a note). 2. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ:• SCF converged after 6 cycles.• Geometry optimization converged successfully.• Frequency (Hessian) calculation completed successfully.• Note: \"Only C1 symmetry has been detected, increase convergence thresholds\". 3. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ:• SCF converged after 6 cycles.• Geometry optimization converged successfully.• Frequency (Hessian) calculation completed successfully.• Note: \"Only C1 symmetry has been detected, increase convergence thresholds\". 4. tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ:• SCF converged after 6 cycles.• Geometry optimization converged successfully.• Frequency (Hessian) calculation completed successfully.• Note: \"Only C1 symmetry has been detected, increase convergence thresholds\". 5. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ:• SCF converged after 6 cycles.• Geometry optimization converged successfully.• Frequency (Hessian) calculation completed successfully.• Note: \"Only C1 symmetry has been detected, increase convergence thresholds\". No errors or critical warnings were found. All calculations terminated normally and completed both geometry optimization and frequency calculations successfully. The only repeated note is about C1 symmetry and convergence thresholds, which is informational and not an error.perform_dft_calculation Action: Tool used:Tool Name: update_global_memory Arguments: log_content: Completed geometry optimization with frequency calculations (OPT FREQ) for five Ce complex conformers using ORCA with the PBE0 functional, def2-SVP basis set, def2-ECP for Ce, D4 dispersion, and RIJCOSX approximation. All calculations converged successfully.perform_dft_calculation Action: I have successfully completed the geometry optimization with frequency calculations for all five Ce complex conformers using ORCA. Here's a summary of what was done: 1. Created job folders for each conformer with appropriate naming: • cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ • tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ • tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ • capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ • capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ 2. Generated ORCA input files with the specified settings: • Functional: PBE0 • Basis set: def2-SVP with def2-ECP for Ce • Dispersion: D4 • RIJCOSX approximation • DEFGRID2 integration grid • SCF autotraH: OFF • 16 cores per calculation • Max iterations: 500 for both SCF and geometry optimization • Charge: 0, Multiplicity: 2 3. Run all calculations in parallel using ORCA 4. Verified the results:• All five calculations completed successfully with \"ORCA TERMINATED NORMALLY\" • Geometry optimizations converged successfully for all conformers • Frequency calculations completed successfully for all conformers • No critical errors were found in any calculation The output files for each calculation are available in their respective job folders, including the optimized geometries (.xyz files) and full output files (.out) containing the calculated properties. Tool Name: remove_imaginary_frequency Arguments: message2agent: Please check for and remove imaginary frequencies from all five Ce complex conformers' DFT optimizations: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ 2. tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ 3. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ 5. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ remove_imaginary_frequency Action: I'll check for and remove imaginary frequencies from all five Ce complex conformers. First, let's check if any of the optimized structures have imaginary frequencies.","level":1,"path_id":"1~78","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":92,"section_id":"sec-1~78"},{"title":"Tool used:","text":"Tool Name: check_imaginary_frequency Arguments:output_file_path_list: ['cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.out', 'tri_-tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ/tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ.out', 'tricapped_trigonal_pris-matic_OPT_FREQ/tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ.out', 'capped_square_antipris-matic_0_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ.out', 'capped_square_an-tiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ.out'] remove_imaginary_frequency Action:Based on the check, I've found imaginary frequencies in two of the conformers: 1. cn9_YICLED_-OPT_FREQ: Imaginary frequency of -131.99 cm -¹ in mode 6 2. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ: Imaginary frequency of -81.23 cm -¹ in mode 6The other three conformers (tri_tri_mer_capped, tricapped_trigonal_prismatic, and capped_square_-antiprismatic_0) don't have imaginary frequencies. Let's now generate trajectory files for the two structures with imaginary frequencies:Tool used: Tool Name: generate_trajectory_files Arguments: output_file_path_list: ['cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.out', 'capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_-FREQ.out'] number_of_mode_list: [6, 6] remove_imaginary_frequency Action: Now, I'll parse the trajectory files to generate distorted xyz coordinates along the imaginary modes: Tool used: Tool Name: parse_distored_xyz_image Arguments: xyz_trajectory_file_names: ['cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ.out.v006.xyz', 'capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antipris-matic_1_OPT_FREQ.out.v006.xyz'] distored_xyz_file_names: ['cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz', 'capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz'] remove_imaginary_frequency Action: Now I'll submit the distorted structures for new geometry optimization and frequency calculations to eliminate the imaginary frequencies: Tool used:Tool Name: perform_dft_calculation Arguments: message2agent: Please make sure to read the cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ.inp and capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ.inp files and use the exact same configuration for the new calculations, simply replace the xyz file in the geometry line. The new calculations are about performing opt and freq calculation with the cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz and capped_square_-antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz files. Please name the output files with a \"_removed\" suffix. I'll perform the new calculations using the distorted geometries while maintaining the exact same ORCA configuration from the original input files. Let me first read the existing input files to understand their configuration:Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please read the following input files and show their contents: 1. cn9_YICLED_-OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.inp 2. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/-capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ.inp interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.inp Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ.inp interact_with_os_and_files Action: The contents of the requested input files are as follows: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.inp ! OPT FREQ PBE0 def2-SVP D4 RIJCOSX DEFGRID2 TightSCF \\%maxcore 4000 \\%pal nprocs 16 end \\%basis Basis \"def2-SVP\" ECP \"def2-ECP\" end \\%scf AutotraH false MaxIter 500 end \\%geom MaxIter 500 coordsys redundant cartfallback true ReducePrint true end \\%output Print[ P\\_Basis ] 2 Print[ P\\_MOs ] 1 Print[P\\_hirshfeld] 1 end * xyzfile 0 2 cn9\\_YICLED\\_0\\_nunpairedes\\_0\\_charge\\_0\\_xtb.xyz 2. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_-FREQ.inp ! OPT FREQ PBE0 def2-SVP D4 RIJCOSX DEFGRID2 TightSCF \\%maxcore 4000 \\%pal nprocs 16 end \\%basis Basis \"def2-SVP\" ECP \"def2-ECP\" end \\%scf AutotraH false MaxIter 500 end \\%geom MaxIter 500 coordsys redundant cartfallback true ReducePrint true end \\%output Print[ P\\_Basis ] 2 Print[ P\\_MOs ] 1 Print[P\\_hirshfeld] 1 end * xyzfile 0 2 capped\\_square\\_antiprismatic\\_1\\_nunpairedes\\_0\\_charge\\_0\\_xtb.xyz Both files contain ORCA input for geometry optimization and frequency calculations using the PBE0 functional, def2-SVP basis set, D4 dispersion, and other specified settings. perform_dft_calculation Action: Now I'll create the new input files for the distorted geometries while maintaining the exact same configuration as the original files: Tool used: Tool Name: input_file_service Arguments: message2agent: Please create two new ORCA input files: 1. In the cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ folder, create cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp • Use the exact same configuration as in cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.inp • But replace the geometry line to use cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz instead 2. In the capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ folder, create capped_square_antipris-matic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp • Use the exact same configuration as in capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ.inp • But replace the geometry line to use capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz instead Keep everything else the same as in the original input files, including all computational parameters and settings. input_file_service Action: Tool used: submit_slurm_job Action: Now I'll check the other input file: Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please check the content of capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/-capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp to verify the number of cores and any dependencies. interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ_removed.inp interact_with_os_and_files Action: The file capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_-FREQ_removed.inp specifies the following regarding cores and dependencies: • Number of cores: 16 (set by %pal nprocs 16) • Memory per core: 4000 MB (set by %maxcore 4000) • SCF autotraH: OFF (set by AutotraH false) • Max SCF and geometry optimization iterations: 500 each • Basis set: def2-SVP with def2-ECP for lanthanoids • Functional: PBE0 • Dispersion: D4 • RIJCOSX approximation and DEFGRID2 grid • TightSCF convergence • Geometry input: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz No external dependencies are referenced beyond the specified basis, ECP, and geometry file. The configuration matches the requirements for a 16-core calculation with the correct settings for a lanthanide complex. submit_slurm_job Action: Based on the verification, both input files: 1. Specify 16 cores (%pal nprocs 16) 2. Use the same level of theory and settings (PBE0/def2-SVP with D4 dispersion) 3. Reference distorted XYZ files as input geometries Now I'll submit both jobs in parallel: Tool used: Tool Name: submit_slurm_jobs Arguments: job_names: ['cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ', 'capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ'] number_of_cores: [16, 16] shell_commands: ['orca cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp &gt; cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ_removed.out', 'orca capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp &gt; capped_-square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.out'] submit_slurm_job Action: I've successfully submitted both ORCA jobs in parallel. Both calculations completed successfully with \"ORCA TERMINATED NORMALLY\" status. Here's a summary: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ (using cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp) • Job ID: 230632 • Status: Completed normally • Cores used: 16 2. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ (using capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_-FREQ_removed.inp) • Job ID: 230633 • Status: Completed normally • Cores used: 16 Both jobs used the specified input files that reference distorted geometries as their starting points. The calculations used PBE0/def2-SVP with D4 dispersion, RIJCOSX approximation, and def2-ECP for lanthanoids, as specified in the input files. Output files (including the main .out file, optimized geometries, and trajectory files) have been generated in their respective directories: -cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.out -cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ_removed.xyz -cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed_trj.xyz -capped_square_antipris-matic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.out -capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.xyz -capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed_trj.xyz The jobs ran smoothly without any errors. run_orca Action: I've successfully run both ORCA jobs in parallel. Here's a summary of the results: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ calculation: • Input file: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp • Job ID: 230632 • Status: Completed successfully (\"ORCA TERMINATED NORMALLY\") • Used 16 cores as specified in the input file • Output files generated: -cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.out (main output) -cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.xyz (optimized structure) -cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed_trj.xyz (trajectory file) 2. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ calculation: • Input file: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp • Job ID: 230633 • Status: Completed successfully (\"ORCA TERMINATED NORMALLY\") • Used 16 cores as specified in the input file • Output files generated: -capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.out (main output) -capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.xyz (optimized structure) -capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed_trj.xyz (trajectory file) Both calculations used PBE0/def2-SVP with D4 dispersion, RIJCOSX approximation, and def2-ECP for lanthanoids (Ce) as specified in the input files. Both jobs started from the distorted geometries provided in the respective _distorted.xyz files. perform_dft_calculation Action:Now, let me check if the new calculations completed successfully:Tool used:Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please check the output files for the new calculations to determine if they converged successfully: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.out 2. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.out For each calculation, look for \"ORCA TERMINATED NORMALLY\" message and verify that both the geometry optimization and frequency calculations completed successfully. Please report if there are any errors or warnings that need attention.interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used:Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.out query_keys: ['convergence_information'] interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ_removed.out query_keys: ['convergence_information'] interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.out Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ_removed.out interact_with_os_and_files Action:Both output files were checked for convergence and errors:1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.out:• SCF converged successfully.• Geometry optimization completed and converged.• No critical errors or warnings, except a note: \"Only C1 symmetry has been detected, increase convergence thresholds\" (not critical for convergence). 2. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_-FREQ_removed.out:• SCF converged successfully.• Geometry optimization completed and converged.• No critical errors or warnings, except a note: \"Only C1 symmetry has been detected, increase convergence thresholds\" (not critical for convergence). Both calculations converged successfully for both geometry optimization and frequency steps. No significant errors or warnings require attention. 1. I read the original input files to ensure I used the exact same configuration:• cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.inp • capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ.inp 2. Created new input files with the \"_removed\" suffix while maintaining all computational parameters:• cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp • capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ_removed.inp 3. Updated the geometry lines in the new input files to use the distorted geometries:• Changed to use cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz • Changed to use capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz 4. Ran both ORCA calculations in parallel using the same settings:• PBE0 functional • def2-SVP basis set with def2-ECP for Ce • D4 dispersion correction • RIJCOSX approximation • DEFGRID2 integration grid • 16 cores per calculation 5. Verified that both calculations completed successfully:• Both calculations converged for both geometry optimization and frequency parts • Output files were generated with the \"_removed\" suffix as requested • No critical errors or warnings were found The calculations were completed successfully and all specified output files are now available in their respective folders.remove_imaginary_frequency Action: Now, let's check if the new calculations still have any imaginary frequencies: Tool used: Tool Name: check_imaginary_frequency Arguments: output_file_path_list: ['cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.out', 'capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_-1_OPT_FREQ_removed.out'] remove_imaginary_frequency Action:I notice that both structures still have imaginary frequencies after the first optimization round: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.out: Imaginary frequency of -85.19 cm -¹ in mode 6 2. capped_-square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.out: Imaginary frequency of -14.79 cm -¹ in mode 6 For the capped_square_antiprismatic_1 conformer, the imaginary frequency is now -14.79 cm -¹, which is less than 15 cm -¹ in absolute value. According to our criteria, this can be properly ignored, so we don't need to optimize this structure further. However, the cn9_YICLED conformer still has a significant imaginary frequency of -85.19 cm -¹, so we need to perform another round of optimization for this structure. Let's generate the trajectory file for the remaining structure with significant imaginary frequency:Tool used: Tool Name: generate_trajectory_files Arguments: output_file_path_list: ['cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.out'] number_of_mode_list: [6] remove_imaginary_frequency Action: Now let's parse the new distorted xyz coordinates for the cn9_YICLED conformer: Tool used: Tool Name: parse_distored_xyz_image Arguments: xyz_trajectory_file_names: ['cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.out.v006.xyz'] distored_xyz_file_names: ['cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_re-moved_distorted.xyz'] remove_imaginary_frequency Action:Now, let's perform a second round of optimization for the cn9_YICLED conformer:Tool used:Tool Name: perform_dft_calculation Arguments: message2agent: Please make sure to read the cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ_removed.inp file and use the exact same configuration for the new calculation, simply replace the xyz file in the geometry line. The new calculation is about performing opt and freq calculation with the cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed_distorted.xyz file. Please name the output file cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.out. Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp interact_with_os_and_files Action: The contents of cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp are: ! OPT FREQ PBE0 def2-SVP D4 RIJCOSX DEFGRID2 TightSCF \\%maxcore 4000 \\%pal nprocs 16 end \\%basis Basis \"def2-SVP\" ECP \"def2-ECP\" end \\%scf AutotraH false MaxIter 500 end \\%geom MaxIter 500 coordsys redundant cartfallback true ReducePrint true end \\%output Print[ P\\_Basis ] 2 Print[ P\\_MOs ] 1 Print[P\\_hirshfeld] 1 end * xyzfile 0 2 cn9\\_YICLED\\_OPT\\_FREQ\\_distorted.xyz perform_dft_calculation Action: Now, I'll create a new input file for the new calculation with the exact same configuration, only changing the geometry file: Tool used: Tool Name: input_file_service Arguments: message2agent: Please create a new ORCA input file in the cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ folder named cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.inp. Use exactly the same configuration as in cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp, but replace the geometry line to use cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed_distorted.xyz instead of cn9_YICLED_-OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz. Keep all other computational parameters exactly the same as in the original input file. input_file_service Action: Tool used: Tool Name: generate_geometry_line Arguments: message2agent: generate the geometry line for cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed_distorted.xyz, the molecule(s) are Ce(III) complex with 3 nitrate_bi and 3 water ligands, the charge and multiplicity are 0 2 generate_geometry_line Action: • xyzfile 0 2 cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed_distorted.xyz input_file_service Action: Tool used: Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity... input_file_service Action:The new ORCA input file cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.inp has been created in the cn9_-YICLED_OPT_FREQ folder. It uses exactly the same configuration as cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ_removed.inp, but the geometry line now references cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed_distorted.xyz:• Geometry line: * xyzfile 0 2 cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed_distorted.xyz • All other computational parameters and settings are identical to the original input file.• The new input file is dependent on cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_-removed_distorted.xyz. The file has been saved as cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.inp. The core specifications are unchanged from the previous input, and the only modification is the geometry file reference.  I'll run the ORCA job as requested. First, let me check the input file to determine the resource requirements and any dependencies:Tool used:Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please check the number of cores required for the ORCA calculation in cn9_-YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.inp and identify any dependent files it may reference. interact_with_os_and_files described above, and each input file is dependent on its respective xyz file.perform_dft_calculation Action: Now I'll run the ORCA single point calculations for all five conformers: Tool used: Tool Name: run_orca Arguments: message2agent: Please run the following ORCA jobs in parallel: 1. Run the job in cn9_YICLED_SP folder using the input file cn9_YICLED_SP.inp 2. Run the job in tri_tri_mer_capped_SP folder using the input file tri_tri_mer_capped_SP.inp 3. Run the job in tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP folder using the input file tricapped_trigonal_-prismatic_SP.inp 4. Run the job in capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP folder using the input file capped_square_-antiprismatic_0_SP.inp 5. Run the job in capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP folder using the input file capped_square_-antiprismatic_1_SP.inp run_orca Action: I'll run the five ORCA single point (SP) jobs in parallel. First, let me check the input files to determine resource requirements and dependencies: Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please check the number of cores required for the ORCA calculations in the following input files and identify any dependent files: 1. cn9_YICLED_SP/cn9_YICLED_SP.inp 2. tri_-tri_mer_capped_SP/tri_tri_mer_capped_SP.inp 3. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP/tricapped_-trigonal_prismatic_SP.inp 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP/capped_square_antiprismatic_-0_SP.inp 5. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP.inp interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_SP/cn9_YICLED_SP.inp Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: tri_tri_mer_capped_SP/tri_tri_mer_capped_SP.inp Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP/tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP.inp Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP.inp file are as previously described, with the only change being the removal of \"VV10\" from the keyword line. run_orca Action: Now I'll resubmit the five ORCA single point calculation jobs with the fixed input files: Tool used:Tool Name: submit_slurm_job Arguments: message2agent: Please submit the following ORCA jobs in parallel with the corrected input files (with VV10 keyword removed): 1. cn9_YICLED_SP folder using the input file cn9_YICLED_SP.inp 2. tri_tri_mer_capped_SP folder using the input file tri_tri_mer_capped_SP.inp 3. tricapped_-trigonal_prismatic_SP folder using the input file tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP.inp 4. capped_-square_antiprismatic_0_SP folder using the input file capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP.inp 5. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP folder using the input file capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-SP.inp Each job requires 16 cores as specified in the input files.submit_slurm_job Action:I'll submit the ORCA single-point jobs with the corrected input files. First, let me check if the VV10 keyword has been removed from the input files:Tool used:Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please check the content of cn9_YICLED_SP/cn9_YICLED_SP.inp to verify if the VV10 keyword has been removed.interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_SP/cn9_YICLED_SP.inp interact_with_os_and_files Action:The content of cn9_YICLED_SP/cn9_YICLED_SP.inp does not contain the VV10 keyword. The VV10 keyword has been removed.","level":1,"path_id":"1~79","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":93,"section_id":"sec-1~79"},{"title":"submit_slurm_job","text":"• Energy check signals convergence. 2. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP.out:• SCF converged after 75 cycles.• Energy check signals convergence. 3. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP.out:• SCF converged after 76 cycles.• Gradient check signals convergence. 4. tri_tri_mer_capped_SP/tri_tri_mer_capped_SP.out:• SCF converged after 71 cycles.• Energy check signals convergence. 5. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP/tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP.out:• SCF converged after 68 cycles.• Energy check signals convergence. No errors or warnings requiring attention were found in the convergence information for any of the calculations. All calculations terminated normally and SCF cycles converged as expected.","level":1,"path_id":"1~80","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":94,"section_id":"sec-1~80"},{"title":"Figure 3","text":"3Figure 3 Hierarchical architecture of El Agente Q. Inter-agent communication is illustrated with black arrows, where solid arrows represent task delegation from higher-level agents, and dotted arrows indicate feedback and reporting from lower-level agents.The agents can be broadly categorized into three functional modules -the geometry module (coral), the quantum calculation module (blue), and the file I/O module (yellow). Each module can be thought of as a department in a complex organization with its own manager, employees, and tools (violet). For example, the geometry generation agent gets to decide if the system should be handled by the organometallic complexes agent or Perplexity AI search (78), and decide the charge and multiplicity of the system.","level":1,"path_id":"1~81","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":95,"section_id":"sec-1~81"},{"title":"Figure 4 .","text":"4This expansion includes the incorporation of user-friendly interfaces-such as enabling users to draw and manipulate molecular structures directly, similar to the functionality provided by tools like JMol (90; 91), ChemDoodle (92), and ChemDraw (93)-thereby making the system more flexible and accessible to a broader range of academic and industrial researchers (Stage 2). Another key development in the initial stage of El Agente Q will be the expansion of its current toolkit to include spectroscopic property predictions, reaction mechanisms, photochemical processes, and high-level single-and multi-reference electronic structure methods as well as excited state methods, such as DLPNO-CCSD(T) (94), MC-PDFT (95), ADC(2) (96; 97) and NEVPT2 (98) (Stage 3).","level":1,"path_id":"1~82","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":96,"section_id":"sec-1~82"},{"title":"Figure 4","text":"4Figure 4 El Agente Q Development Roadmap: Bridging Molecular Properties, Advanced Simulations, and Self-Driving Lab Integration. The milestones are not drawn to timescale.","level":1,"path_id":"1~83","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":97,"section_id":"sec-1~83"},{"title":"2 :","text":"2Organize files and perform geometry optimizations\nNow I'll perform parallel geometry optimizations using ORCA with HF/def2-SVP:Tool used:","level":1,"path_id":"1~84","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":98,"section_id":"sec-1~84"},{"title":"3 :","text":"3Generate reports for each moleculeNow I'll generate individual reports for each molecule by extracting the requested properties from the output files.","level":1,"path_id":"1~85","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":99,"section_id":"sec-1~85"},{"title":"4 :","text":"4Create Comprehensive ReportsNow I'll combine all the extracted information into individual comprehensive reports for each molecule.Tool used:Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity... computational_chemist Action:\nxyz 3. L-phenylalanine_zwitterion/L-phenylalanine_zwitterion_HF_OPT_FREQ/L-phenylalanine_zwitterion_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz 4. 2-chloronitrobenzene/2-chloronitrobenzene_HF_OPT_FREQ/2-chloronitrobenzene_HF_-OPT_FREQ.xyz 5. cis-1_2-cyclohexanediol/cis-1_2-cyclohexanediol_HF_OPT_FREQ/cis-1_2-cyclohexanediol_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz 6. L-histidine_non_zwitterion/L-histidine_non_zwitterion_HF_OPT_FREQ/L-histidine_-non_zwitterion_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz 7. 2_2-biphenol/2_2-biphenol_HF_OPT_FREQ/2_2-biphenol_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz 8. S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol/S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol_HF_OPT_FREQ/S-2-ethyl-2fluoropentan-1-ol_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz 9. R-3-hydroxycyclopentan-1-one/R-3-hydroxycyclopentan-1-one_HF_OPT_FREQ/R-3hydroxycyclopentan-1-one_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz 10. 3-methylbutanoate_anion/3-methylbutanoate_anion_HF_OPT_FREQ/3-methylbutanoate_-anion_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz 11. diisopropylamide_anion/diisopropylamide_anion_HF_OPT_FREQ/diisopropylamide_an-ion_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz 12. diisopropylammonium_cation/diisopropylammonium_cation_HF_OPT_FREQ/diisopropy-lammonium_cation_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz computational_chemist Action:\nll continue with the process of generating reports for the remaining molecules. Let's proceed with L-phenylalanine_zwitterion: Please extract the following information from the L-phenylalanine_zwitterion/L-phenylalanine_zwitterion_HF_OPT_FREQ/L-phenylalanine_zwitterion_HF_OPT_FREQ.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energy table 6. HOMO-LUMO gap 7. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld charges)Please format the output in markdown format.\ncontinue generating reports for the remaining molecules, focusing now on 2-chloronitrobenzene: Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please extract the following information from the 2-chloronitrobenzene/2-chloronitrobenzene_HF_OPT_FREQ/2-chloronitrobenzene_HF_OPT_FREQ.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energy table 6. HOMO-LUMO gap 7. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld charges) Please format the output in markdown format.\ncontinue extracting data and creating reports for the remaining molecules. Let's proceed with cis-1_2-cyclohexanediol: Please extract the following information from the cis-1_2-cyclohexanediol/cis-1_-2-cyclohexanediol_HF_OPT_FREQ/cis-1_2-cyclohexanediol_HF_OPT_FREQ.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energy table 6. HOMO-LUMO gap 7. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld charges)Please format the output in markdown format.\ncontinue with extracting data and creating reports for the remaining molecules. Let's proceed with L-histidine_non_zwitterion: Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please extract the following information from the L-histidine_non_zwitterion/L-histidine_non_zwitterion_HF_OPT_FREQ/L-histidine_non_zwitterion_HF_OPT_FREQ.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energy table 6. HOMO-LUMO gap 7. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld charges) Please format the output in markdown format.\nNow I'll create the report for L-histidine_non_zwitterion:Tool used:Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity...\nPlease extract the following information from the 2_2-biphenol/2_2-biphenol_-HF_OPT_FREQ/2_2-biphenol_HF_OPT_FREQ.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energy table 6. HOMO-LUMO gap 7. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld charges) Please format the output in markdown format.\nLet's get the charge information from the Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld files for 2_2-biphenol:Tool used:Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please read the contents of these three charge analysis files: 1. 2_2-biphenol/2_-2-biphenol_HF_OPT_FREQ/2_2-biphenol_HF_OPT_FREQ_mulliken_charges.md 2.2_2biphenol/2_2-biphenol_HF_OPT_FREQ/2_2-biphenol_HF_OPT_FREQ_loewdin_charges.md 3. 2_2-biphenol/2_2-biphenol_HF_OPT_FREQ/2_2-biphenol_HF_OPT_FREQ_hirshfeld_charges.md computational_chemist Action:Now let me create the report for 2_2-biphenol:Tool used:Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity...\ncontinue extracting data and generating reports for the remaining molecules. Let's extract data for S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol: Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please extract the following information from the S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol/S-2ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol_HF_OPT_FREQ/S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol_HF_OPT_FREQ.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energy table 6. HOMO-LUMO gap 7. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld charges)Please format the output in markdown format.\nto get the charge data for S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol:Tool used:Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please read the contents of these three charge analysis files: 1. S-2-ethyl-2fluoropentan-1-ol/S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol_HF_OPT_FREQ/S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol_-HF_OPT_FREQ_mulliken_charges.md 2. S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol/S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol_HF_OPT_FREQ/S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol_HF_OPT_FREQ_loewdin_charges.md 3.S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol/S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol_HF_OPT_FREQ/S-2-ethyl-2fluoropentan-1-ol_HF_OPT_FREQ_hirshfeld_charges.md computational_chemist Action:Let me create the report for S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol:Tool used:Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments:code: omitted for brevity...\nNow I'll create the report for 3-methylbutanoate_anion:Tool used:Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity...\nGenerate the initial geometries for the following molecules using their SMILES representations: 1. Chromium(0) hexacarbonyl (low spin): Cr(=C=O)(=C=O)(=C=O)(=C=O)(=C=O) 2. Chlorine trifluoride: FCl(F)F 3. Fluorophosphoric acid (singly deprotonated form): [O-]P(F)(O)=O computational_chemist Action: Now I'll organize the files into appropriate folders and perform parallel geometry optimizations using ORCA with the specified parameters (HF/def2-SVP in gas phase): folder: Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please create three separate folders for each molecule and move the initial geometry files into their respective folders: 1. Create a folder named \"Cr_hexacarbonyl\" and move chromium_-hexacarbonyl_initial.xyz into it 2. Create a folder named \"ClF3\" and move chlorine_trifluoride_initial.xyz into it 3. Create a folder named \"fluorophosphoric_acid\" and move fluorophosphoric_acid_-deprotonated_initial.xyz into it computational_chemist Action: Now I'll perform the parallel geometry optimizations using ORCA with the HF/def2-SVP method: Tool used:Tool Name: geometry_optimization Arguments: message2agent: Perform parallel geometry optimization for all three molecules using ORCA with the Hartree-Fock (HF) method and def2-SVP basis set in the gas phase. For each molecule:1. Chromium(0) hexacarbonyl (in Cr_hexacarbonyl folder):• Input file: chromium_hexacarbonyl_initial.xyz • Charge: 0 • Multiplicity: 1 (low spin) 2. Chlorine trifluoride (in ClF3 folder):• Input file: chlorine_trifluoride_initial.xyz • Charge: 0 • Multiplicity: 1 3. Fluorophosphoric acid (in fluorophosphoric_acid folder):• Input file: fluorophosphoric_acid_deprotonated_initial.xyz • Charge: -1 • Multiplicity: 1 Use ORCA as the QM software with HF/def2-SVP in the gas phase for all calculations. Please make sure to calculate all properties needed for the report (point group symmetry, dipole moment, molecular orbital analysis, and atomic charge analysis including Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld).\nNow I'll generate detailed reports for each molecule. I'll start by extracting all the necessary data from the output files:Tool used:Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: For the first molecule (chromium hexacarbonyl), please extract the following information from the Cr_hexacarbonyl/chromium_hexacarbonyl_opt.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energy table 6. HOMO-LUMO gap 7. Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld atomic charges Save each section to separate markdown files in the Cr_hexacarbonyl folder.\nll create a report for the first molecule (chromium hexacarbonyl):Tool used:Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity...\nPlease visualize the optimized structure of fluorophosphoric acid (deprotonated form) from the file fluorophosphoric_acid/fluorophosphoric_acid_deprotonated_opt.xyz computational_chemist Action: Summary of Completed Tasks I've successfully completed the parallel geometry optimizations of three inorganic compounds using ORCA with the Hartree-Fock method and def2-SVP basis set in the gas phase. Here's a summary of what was accomplished:\nOptimization and AnalysisI'll execute the following steps to complete this request:1. Organize the files by creating folders for each molecule 2. Perform parallel geometry optimization with ORCA using HF/def2-SVP 3. Generate detailed reports for each molecule with the requested informationStep 1: Organizing the filesTool used:","level":1,"path_id":"1~86","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":100,"section_id":"sec-1~86"},{"title":"2 :Step 3 :","text":"23Performing Parallel Geometry OptimizationNow I'll submit all 16 molecules for geometry optimization using ORCA with HF/def2-SVP:Tool used:Tool Name: geometry_optimization Arguments: message2agent: Please optimize the following molecules in parallel using ORCA with the Hartree-Fock (HF) method and def2-SVP basis set in the gas phase. For each molecule, I'm providing the folder name, charge, and multiplicity:1. chromium_hexacarbonyl, charge = 0, multiplicity = 1 2. chlorine_trifluoride, charge = 0, multiplicity = 1 3. fluorophosphoric_acid_singly_deprotonated_form, charge = -1, multiplicity = 1 4. trifluoromethane_sulfonate, charge = -1, multiplicity = 1 5. cyclohexyldimethylphosphine, charge = 0, multiplicity = 1 6. t-butylisothiocyanate, charge = 0, multiplicity = 1 7. chromic_acid, charge = 0, multiplicity = 1 8. permanganic_acid, charge = 0, multiplicity = 1 9. perchlorate, charge = -1, multiplicity = 1 10. hexafluorophosphate, charge = -1, multiplicity = 1 11. tetrafluoroborate, charge = -1, multiplicity = 1 12. dicyanoaurate, charge = -1, multiplicity = 1 13. nitrogen_trifluoride, charge = 0, multiplicity = 1 14. sulfur_hexafluoride, charge = 0, multiplicity = 1 15. sulfur_tetrafluoride, charge = 0, multiplicity = 1 16. xenon_tetrafluoride, charge = 0, multiplicity = 1 computational_chemist Action:Generating Individual ReportsNow I'll generate individual reports for each molecule with all the requested information. I'll create reports one by one, starting with chromium hexacarbonyl: Please extract the following information from the chromium_hexacarbonyl_HF_-OPT_FREQ/chromium_hexacarbonyl.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energies (enough to determine HOMO-LUMO gap) 6. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld) Please visualize the optimized structure of chromium_hexacarbonyl in the chromium_hexacarbonyl_HF_OPT_FREQ folder. Use the final optimized geometry file (not the trajectory file).\nNow let's continue with the second molecule: chlorine trifluoride. I'll follow the same process.\nPlease extract the following information from the chlorine_trifluoride_HF_OPT_-FREQ/chlorine_trifluoride.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energies (enough to determine HOMO-LUMO gap) 6. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld) Please visualize the optimized structure of chlorine_trifluoride in the chlorine_-trifluoride_HF_OPT_FREQ folder. Use the final optimized geometry file. omitted for brevity...\ncontinue with the third molecule: fluorophosphoric acid (singly deprotonated form).\nPlease extract the following information from the fluorophosphoric_acid_singly_-deprotonated_form_HF_OPT_FREQ/fluorophosphoric_acid_singly_deprotonated_form.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry (if available) 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energies (enough to determine HOMO-LUMO gap) 6. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld) Please visualize the optimized structure of fluorophosphoric_acid_singly_deproto-nated_form in the fluorophosphoric_acid_singly_deprotonated_form_HF_OPT_FREQ folder. Use the final optimized geometry file.\nomitted for brevity...\nLet me try to visualize the xenon tetrafluoride using a different approach:Tool used:Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please check the content of the xenon_tetrafluoride_HF_OPT_FREQ/xenon_tetrafluoride.xyz file and fix it if necessary. Then try to visualize the structure again.\nLet me try a different approach to generate an image for xenon tetrafluoride: Tool used: Tool Name: geometry_visualization Arguments: message2agent: Please generate a visualization for the xenon_tetrafluoride molecule from the xenon_tetrafluoride_HF_OPT_FREQ/xenon_tetrafluoride.xyz file. about the electronic structure and properties of each compound. Would you like me to provide any specific additional analysis or comparisons between the molecules? B.4 Relative Stability of Carbocations B.4.1 Level 1\nNow, let's optimize the carbocations using DFT with B3LYP/6-31G*:Tool used:Tool Name: geometry_optimization code: omitted for brevity...\nNow I need to organize the generated files and set up for geometry optimization:\nto calculate the reaction energies for cyclo(CnH2n) → cyclo(Cn-1H2n-3)-CH3 reactions. Let's extract the energies from the output files:Tool used:\nNow I'll create a final report based on the post-analysis results:Tool used:","level":1,"path_id":"1~87","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":101,"section_id":"sec-1~87"},{"title":"2 :","text":"2Optimize geometries with DFT using B3LYP/6-31G* and CPCM water solvation Tool used:","level":1,"path_id":"1~88","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":102,"section_id":"sec-1~88"},{"title":"3 :","text":"3Calculate the pKa value using the computational results","level":1,"path_id":"1~89","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":103,"section_id":"sec-1~89"},{"title":"3 :","text":"3Extract Gibbs free energies for all molecules Tool used:","level":1,"path_id":"1~90","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":104,"section_id":"sec-1~90"},{"title":"Step 4 :","text":"4Calculate ∆G of deprotonation for all acids and calibrate the proton solvation energyLet me calculate ∆G of deprotonation and calibrate the proton solvation energy:Tool used:\nsuccessfully completed the TDDFT calculations for structures 2, 3, and 5 as requested. All calculations were performed using RI-wB2PLYP/def2-mSVP with AutoAux on neutral systems (charge=0, multiplicity=1).\ncompleted all the requested tasks. Here's a summary of what was done:","level":1,"path_id":"1~91","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":105,"section_id":"sec-1~91"},{"title":"1 .","text":"1Document computational details in global memory 2. Generate initial geometry for Ce complex with 3 nitrate_bi and 3 water ligands 3. Optimize geometry using PBE0/def2-SVP with specified ORCA settings 4. Perform single point calculations with ωB97M-V/def2-SVPD on optimized geometries 5. Extract and report energies for all conformers Executing Plan First, I'll document the computational details in global memory: Tool used: Tool Name: update_global_memory Arguments: log_content: Lanthanide complex study requirements: -Complex: Ce metal core, +3 oxidation state, CN=9 -Ligands: 3 nitrate_bi and 3 water -Charge: 0, Multiplicity: 2 -Geometry optimization: PBE0/def2-SVP with D4 dispersion, RIJCOSX, DEFGRID2 -Single point: ωB97M-V/def2-SVPD with VV10 dispersion -def2-ECP for lanthanoids -SCF autotraH: OFF for both calculations -Using 16 cores per calculation -Convergence max iterations: 500+ for both opt and SCF -TightSCF or extreme tight settings required computational_chemist Action:\ngenerated the initial geometries for the Ce complex according to the specifications. Since Ce is a lanthanide metal (element 58), I used the specialized metal complex geometry generation tool. The system generated 5 different conformers of the complex with the following characteristics: -Metal center: Ce (Cerium) -Oxidation state: +3 -Coordination number: 9 -Ligands: 3 nitrate_bi ligands and 3 water ligands -Complex charge: 0 (neutral) -Multiplicity: 2 The generated conformers represent different possible arrangements of the ligands around the Ce(III) center: 1. cn9_YICLED_0_nunpairedes_0_charge_0.xyz 2. tri_tri_mer_capped_0_nunpairedes_-0_charge_0.xyz 3. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_1_nunpairedes_0_charge_0.xyz 4. capped_-square_antiprismatic_0_nunpairedes_0_charge_0.xyz 5. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_nun-pairedes_0_charge_0.xyz Let me update the global memory with this information:Tool used:Tool Name: update_global_memory Arguments: log_content: Ce complex system profile: -Metal: Ce(III) -Coordination number: 9 -Ligands: 3 nitrate_bi + 3 water -Charge: 0 -Multiplicity: 2 -Conformers generated: 5 (cn9_YICLED, tri_tri_mer_capped, tricapped_trigonal_prismatic, and two capped_square_antiprismatic variants)\ncustom ORCA %basis block for a Ce(III) complex. -All atoms use def2-SVP basis set. -Ce uses def2-ECP effective core potential. Optimized %basis block: %basis Basis \"def2-SVP\" ECP \"def2-ECP\" end This block ensures all atoms use def2-SVP, and Ce uses the def2-ECP as required. No unnecessary auxiliary or decontraction options were included. Configuration_Recommender Action: PBE0 def2-SVP global_memory_expert • 16 cores (%pal nprocs 16) • Global memory: %maxcore 4000 • Output block included as per best practice • Charge: 0, Multiplicity: 2 (geometry line references the correct xyz file for each conformer) The input files and their dependencies are: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ.inp (depends on cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_0_nunpairedes_0_charge_-0_xtb.xyz) 2. tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ/tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ.inp (depends on tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ/tri_tri_mer_capped_0_nunpairedes_0_charge_0_xtb.xyz) 3. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ/tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_-FREQ.inp (depends on tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ/tricapped_trigonal_pris-matic_1_nunpairedes_0_charge_0_xtb.xyz) 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ/-capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ.inp (depends on capped_square_antiprismatic_0_-OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_nunpairedes_0_charge_0_xtb.xyz) 5. capped_-square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ.inp (depends on capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_nunpairedes_-0_charge_0_xtb.xyz) All files have been stored in the correct directories. The core specifications for each input file are as described above, and each input file is dependent on its respective xyz file. perform_dft_calculation Action: Now I'll run the ORCA calculations for all five conformers: Tool used:\nThe ORCA input file cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.inp produced an error: 'Unknown identifier in SCF block line 13: Last token: TIGHTSCF.' The SCF block used was: %scf AutotraH false MaxIter 500 TightSCF true end . Please provide a corrected SCF block for a Ce(III) complex with SCF autotraH OFF, max iterations 500, and TightSCF convergence criteria.\nHere is the corrected SCF block for your ORCA input: %scf AutotraH false MaxIter 500 ConvCriteria Tight end This uses ConvCriteria Tight, which is the correct keyword for tight SCF convergence in ORCA.\ngenerate the geometry line for cn9_YICLED_0_nunpairedes_0_charge_0_xtb.xyz, the molecule(s) are Ce(III) complex with 3 nitrate_bi and 3 water ligands, the charge and multiplicity are\nomitted for brevity...\nNow I'll resubmit the fixed jobs in parallel:Tool used:\nNow I'll check if all the calculations completed successfully:Tool used:","level":1,"path_id":"1~92","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":106,"section_id":"sec-1~92"},{"title":"3 :","text":"3Check for and Remove Imaginary FrequenciesNow I'll check for and remove any imaginary frequencies that might be present in the DFT optimized structures:Tool used:\nCompleted additional geometry optimization with frequency calculations for two distorted Ce complex conformers (cn9_YICLED and capped_square_antiprismatic_1) using ORCA with the same PBE0/def2-SVP/D4/RIJCOSX settings as the original calculations. Both calculations converged successfully. perform_dft_calculation Action: I've successfully completed the additional geometry optimization and frequency calculations for the distorted Ce complex conformers as requested. Here's a summary of what was done:\ncreate a new calculation based on the exact configuration from the cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp file, changing only the geometry file as requested. Let me first read the existing input file:Please read the cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp file and show its contents.\nNow, I'll run the new ORCA calculation with the created input file:Tool used:Tool Name: run_orca Arguments: message2agent: Please run the ORCA job in the cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ folder using the input file cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.inp. The output file should be named cn9_YICLED_-OPT_FREQ_removed2.out.","level":1,"path_id":"1~93","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":107,"section_id":"sec-1~93"},{"title":"2 .","text":"2Completed single point calculations for all five Ce complex conformers using optimized geometries with ωB97M-V functional and def2-SVPD basis set (def2-ECP for Ce). The VV10 nonlocal dispersion keyword was incompatible with the installed ORCA version, so calculations were performed without it. All calculations converged successfully. perform_dft_calculation Action: I have successfully completed single point calculations for all five Ce complex conformers using their optimized geometries. Here's a summary of what was done:1. Created job folders for each conformer with the \"_SP\" suffix:Copied the optimized geometry files to their respective SP job folders: • cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.xyz to cn9_YICLED_SP/ • tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ.xyz to tri_tri_mer_capped_SP/ • tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ.xyz to tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP/ • capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ.xyz to capped_square_antiprismatic_0_-SP/ • capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.xyz to capped_square_antipris-matic_1_SP/ 3. Created ORCA input files for single point calculations with the specified settings: • Functional: ωB97M-V • Basis set: def2-SVPD with def2-ECP for Ce • SCF autotraH: OFF • TightSCF convergence criteria\n\n\n\n\n\n\t\t\tPronounced /el a'xente/, approximately \"el ah-HEN-teh\" in English. See audio: Zenodo (MP3).\n\t\t\tThis field was left empty due to an API bug; overall performance was unaffected.","level":1,"path_id":"1~94","parent_section_id":null,"order_in_parent":108,"section_id":"sec-1~94"}],"citations":[{"raw_text":"Introduction to Computational Chemistry\n\t\t\n\t\t\tFrankJensen\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tFebruary 2017\n\t\t\tUZOVDQAAQBAJ","doi":null,"arxiv_id":null,"title":null,"anchors":[]},{"raw_text":"Holy grails for computational organic chemistry and biochemistry\n\t\t\n\t\t\tKNHouk\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tFangLiu\n\t\t\n\t\t10.1021/acs.accounts.6b00532\n\t\t\n\t\n\t\n\t\tAccounts of Chemical Research\n\t\t0001-4842\n\t\t\n\t\t\t50\n\t\t\t3\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\tMarch 2017\n\t\t\tAmerican Chemical Society","doi":"10.1021/acs.accounts.6b00532","arxiv_id":null,"title":"Holy grails for computational organic chemistry and biochemistry","anchors":[]},{"raw_text":"Computational Chemistry: A Practical Guide for Applying Techniques to Real World Problems\n\t\t\n\t\t\tDavidYoung\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tMarch 2004\n\t\t\tJohn Wiley &amp; Sons","doi":null,"arxiv_id":null,"title":null,"anchors":[]},{"raw_text":"Computational Chemistry: Introduction to the Theory and Applications of Molecular and Quantum Mechanics\n\t\t\n\t\t\tErrolGLewars\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tNovember 2010\n\t\t\tzwDCWvnbrQwC","doi":null,"arxiv_id":null,"title":null,"anchors":[]},{"raw_text":"The central role of density functional theory in the AI age\n\t\t\n\t\t\tBingHuang\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tGuidoFalk Von Rudorff\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tOAnatole Von Lilienfeld\n\t\t\n\t\t10.1126/science.abn3445\n\t\t\n\t\n\t\n\t\tScience\n\t\t\n\t\t\t381\n\t\t\t6654\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\tJuly 2023\n\t\t\tAmerican Association for the Advancement of Science","doi":"10.1126/science.abn3445","arxiv_id":null,"title":"The central role of density functional theory in the AI age","anchors":[]},{"raw_text":"Identification of unknown inverted singlet-triplet cores by high-throughput virtual screening\n\t\t\n\t\t\tHÖmer\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tXiaoyuOmar\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tAlessandroXie\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tDanieleTroisi\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tPadula\n\t\t\n\t\t10.1021/jacs.3c05452\n\t\t\n\t\n\t\n\t\tJournal of the American Chemical Society\n\t\t0002-7863\n\t\t\n\t\t\t145\n\t\t\t36\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\tSeptember 2023\n\t\t\tAmerican Chemical Society","doi":"10.1021/jacs.3c05452","arxiv_id":null,"title":"Identification of unknown inverted singlet-triplet cores by high-throughput virtual screening","anchors":[]},{"raw_text":"Design of efficient molecular organic light-emitting diodes by a high-throughput virtual screening and experimental approach\n\t\t\n\t\t\tRafaelGómez-Bombarelli\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tJorgeAguilera-Iparraguirre\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tTimothyDHirzel\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tDavidDuvenaud\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tDougalMaclaurin\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tMartinABlood-Forsythe\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tHyunSik Chae\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tMarkusEinzinger\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tDong-GwangHa\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tTonyWu\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tGeorgiosMarkopoulos\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tSoonokJeon\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tHosukKang\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tHiroshiMiyazaki\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tMasakiNumata\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tSunghanKim\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tWenliangHuang\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tSeong IkHong\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tMarcBaldo\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tRyanPAdams\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tAlánAspuru-Guzik\n\t\t\n\t\t10.1038/nmat4717\n\t\t\n\t\n\t\n\t\tNature Materials\n\t\t1476-4660\n\t\t\n\t\t\t15\n\t\t\t10\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\tOctober 2016\n\t\t\tNature Publishing Group","doi":"10.1038/nmat4717","arxiv_id":null,"title":"Design of efficient molecular organic light-emitting diodes by a high-throughput virtual screening and experimental approach","anchors":[]},{"raw_text":"High-throughput virtual screening of organic second-order nonlinear optical chromophores within the donor-π-bridge-acceptor framework\n\t\t\n\t\t\tChunyunTu\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tWeijiangHuang\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tShengLiang\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tKuiWang\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tQinTian\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tWeiYan\n\t\t\n\t\t10.1039/D3CP04046A\n\t\t\n\t\n\t\n\t\tPhysical Chemistry Chemical Physics\n\t\t1463-9084\n\t\t\n\t\t\t26\n\t\t\t3\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\tJanuary 2024\n\t\t\tThe Royal Society of Chemistry","doi":"10.1039/D3CP04046A","arxiv_id":null,"title":"High-throughput virtual screening of organic second-order nonlinear optical chromophores within the donor-π-bridge-acceptor framework","anchors":[]},{"raw_text":"High-throughput virtual screening of potential inhibitors of GPR52 using docking and biased sampling method for huntington&apos;s disease therapy\n\t\t\n\t\t\tHimanshiGupta\n\t\t\n\t\t\n\t\t\tShaktiSahi\n\t\t\n\t\t10.1007/s11030-023-10763-y\n\t\t\n\t\n\t\n\t\tMolecular Diversity\n\t\t\n\t\t\t28\n\t\t\t5\n\t\t\t\n\t\t\tOctober 2024\n\t\t\tSpringer International Publishing\n\t\t\tDistributor; Label","doi":"10.1007/s11030-023-10763-y","arxiv_id":null,"title":"High-throughput virtual screening of potential inhibitors of GPR52 using docking and biased sampling method for huntington&apos;s disease therapy","anchors":[]},{"raw_text":"Computational exploration of novel