EQ.inp \\%scf AutotraH false MaxIter 500 end 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_-FREQ.inp \\%scf AutotraH false MaxIter 500</p><p>• All jobs failed with an error: \"Unknown identifier in SCF block line 13: Last token: TIGHTSCF\" • Fixed the input files by replacing \"TightSCF true\" with \"ConvCriteria Tight\" 3. Second submission attempt:</p><p>• Jobs failed again with error: \"Unknown identifier in SCF block line 13: Last token: CON-VCRITERIA\" • Modified the input files by removing the problematic convergence criteria settings entirely 4. Final successful submission:</p><p>• All five jobs completed successfully with \"ORCA TERMINATED NORMALLY\" status • Each job directory now contains the expected output files (.out, .xyz, _trj.xyz) The following ORCA geometry optimization and frequency calculations for the Ce(III) complexes are now complete: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ 2. tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ 3. tricapped_-trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ 5. capped_-square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ All output files are available in their respective job directories. Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please check the output files for all five calculations to determine if they converged successfully. For each calculation, check the corresponding .out file in the respective folder and look for \"ORCA TERMINATED NORMALLY\" message and verify that both the geometry optimization and frequency calculations completed successfully. Please report if there are any errors or warnings that need attention.</p><p>interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.out query_keys: ['convergence_information'] interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_-OPT_FREQ.out query_keys: ['convergence_information'] Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ.out query_keys: ['convergence_information'] Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ/tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ.out query_keys: ['convergence_information'] Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ/tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_-FREQ.out query_keys: ['convergence_information'] interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.out query_keys: ['convergence_information', 'SCF HESSIAN'] Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_-OPT_FREQ.out query_keys: ['convergence_information', 'SCF HESSIAN'] Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ.out query_keys: ['convergence_information', 'SCF HESSIAN'] Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ/tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ.out query_keys: ['convergence_information', 'SCF HESSIAN'] Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ/tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_-FREQ.out query_keys: ['convergence_information', 'SCF HESSIAN'] interact_with_os_and_files Action:</p><p>All five calculations were checked for convergence and completion of both geometry optimization and frequency (Hessian) calculations. Here are the results: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ:</p><p>• SCF converged after 6 cycles.</p><p>• Geometry optimization converged successfully.</p><p>• Frequency (Hessian) calculation completed successfully.</p><p>• Note: \"Only C1 symmetry has been detected, increase convergence thresholds\" (not a critical error, just a note). 2. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ:</p><p>• SCF converged after 6 cycles.</p><p>• Geometry optimization converged successfully.</p><p>• Frequency (Hessian) calculation completed successfully.</p><p>• Note: \"Only C1 symmetry has been detected, increase convergence thresholds\". 3. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ:</p><p>• SCF converged after 6 cycles.</p><p>• Geometry optimization converged successfully.</p><p>• Frequency (Hessian) calculation completed successfully.</p><p>• Note: \"Only C1 symmetry has been detected, increase convergence thresholds\". 4. tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ:</p><p>• SCF converged after 6 cycles.</p><p>• Geometry optimization converged successfully.</p><p>• Frequency (Hessian) calculation completed successfully.</p><p>• Note: \"Only C1 symmetry has been detected, increase convergence thresholds\". 5. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ:</p><p>• SCF converged after 6 cycles.</p><p>• Geometry optimization converged successfully.</p><p>• Frequency (Hessian) calculation completed successfully.</p><p>• Note: \"Only C1 symmetry has been detected, increase convergence thresholds\". No errors or critical warnings were found. All calculations terminated normally and completed both geometry optimization and frequency calculations successfully. The only repeated note is about C1 symmetry and convergence thresholds, which is informational and not an error.</p><p>perform_dft_calculation Action: Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: update_global_memory Arguments: log_content: Completed geometry optimization with frequency calculations (OPT FREQ) for five Ce complex conformers using ORCA with the PBE0 functional, def2-SVP basis set, def2-ECP for Ce, D4 dispersion, and RIJCOSX approximation. All calculations converged successfully.</p><p>perform_dft_calculation Action: I have successfully completed the geometry optimization with frequency calculations for all five Ce complex conformers using ORCA. Here's a summary of what was done: 1. Created job folders for each conformer with appropriate naming: • cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ • tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ • tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ • capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ • capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ 2. Generated ORCA input files with the specified settings: • Functional: PBE0 • Basis set: def2-SVP with def2-ECP for Ce • Dispersion: D4 • RIJCOSX approximation • DEFGRID2 integration grid • SCF autotraH: OFF • 16 cores per calculation • Max iterations: 500 for both SCF and geometry optimization • Charge: 0, Multiplicity: 2 3. Run all calculations in parallel using ORCA 4. Verified the results:</p><p>• All five calculations completed successfully with \"ORCA TERMINATED NORMALLY\" • Geometry optimizations converged successfully for all conformers • Frequency calculations completed successfully for all conformers • No critical errors were found in any calculation The output files for each calculation are available in their respective job folders, including the optimized geometries (.xyz files) and full output files (.out) containing the calculated properties. Tool Name: remove_imaginary_frequency Arguments: message2agent: Please check for and remove imaginary frequencies from all five Ce complex conformers' DFT optimizations: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ 2. tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ 3. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ 5. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ remove_imaginary_frequency Action: I'll check for and remove imaginary frequencies from all five Ce complex conformers. First, let's check if any of the optimized structures have imaginary frequencies.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Tool used:</head><p>Tool Name: check_imaginary_frequency Arguments:</p><p>output_file_path_list: ['cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.out', 'tri_-tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ/tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ.out', 'tricapped_trigonal_pris-matic_OPT_FREQ/tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ.out', 'capped_square_antipris-matic_0_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ.out', 'capped_square_an-tiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ.out'] remove_imaginary_frequency Action:</p><p>Based on the check, I've found imaginary frequencies in two of the conformers: 1. cn9_YICLED_-OPT_FREQ: Imaginary frequency of -131.99 cm -¹ in mode 6 2. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ: Imaginary frequency of -81.23 cm -¹ in mode 6</p><p>The other three conformers (tri_tri_mer_capped, tricapped_trigonal_prismatic, and capped_square_-antiprismatic_0) don't have imaginary frequencies. Let's now generate trajectory files for the two structures with imaginary frequencies:</p><p>Tool used: Tool Name: generate_trajectory_files Arguments: output_file_path_list: ['cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.out', 'capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_-FREQ.out'] number_of_mode_list: [6, 6] remove_imaginary_frequency Action: Now, I'll parse the trajectory files to generate distorted xyz coordinates along the imaginary modes: Tool used: Tool Name: parse_distored_xyz_image Arguments: xyz_trajectory_file_names: ['cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ.out.v006.xyz', 'capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antipris-matic_1_OPT_FREQ.out.v006.xyz'] distored_xyz_file_names: ['cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz', 'capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz'] remove_imaginary_frequency Action: Now I'll submit the distorted structures for new geometry optimization and frequency calculations to eliminate the imaginary frequencies: Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: perform_dft_calculation Arguments: message2agent: Please make sure to read the cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ.inp and capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ.inp files and use the exact same configuration for the new calculations, simply replace the xyz file in the geometry line. The new calculations are about performing opt and freq calculation with the cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz and capped_square_-antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz files. Please name the output files with a \"_removed\" suffix. I'll perform the new calculations using the distorted geometries while maintaining the exact same ORCA configuration from the original input files. Let me first read the existing input files to understand their configuration:</p><p>Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please read the following input files and show their contents: 1. cn9_YICLED_-OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.inp 2. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/-capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ.inp interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.inp Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ.inp interact_with_os_and_files Action: The contents of the requested input files are as follows: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.inp ! OPT FREQ PBE0 def2-SVP D4 RIJCOSX DEFGRID2 TightSCF \\%maxcore 4000 \\%pal nprocs 16 end \\%basis Basis \"def2-SVP\" ECP \"def2-ECP\" end \\%scf AutotraH false MaxIter 500 end \\%geom MaxIter 500 coordsys redundant cartfallback true ReducePrint true end \\%output Print[ P\\_Basis ] 2 Print[ P\\_MOs ] 1 Print[P\\_hirshfeld] 1 end * xyzfile 0 2 cn9\\_YICLED\\_0\\_nunpairedes\\_0\\_charge\\_0\\_xtb.xyz 2. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_-FREQ.inp ! OPT FREQ PBE0 def2-SVP D4 RIJCOSX DEFGRID2 TightSCF \\%maxcore 4000 \\%pal nprocs 16 end \\%basis Basis \"def2-SVP\" ECP \"def2-ECP\" end \\%scf AutotraH false MaxIter 500 end \\%geom MaxIter 500 coordsys redundant cartfallback true ReducePrint true end \\%output Print[ P\\_Basis ] 2 Print[ P\\_MOs ] 1 Print[P\\_hirshfeld] 1 end * xyzfile 0 2 capped\\_square\\_antiprismatic\\_1\\_nunpairedes\\_0\\_charge\\_0\\_xtb.xyz Both files contain ORCA input for geometry optimization and frequency calculations using the PBE0 functional, def2-SVP basis set, D4 dispersion, and other specified settings. perform_dft_calculation Action: Now I'll create the new input files for the distorted geometries while maintaining the exact same configuration as the original files: Tool used: Tool Name: input_file_service Arguments: message2agent: Please create two new ORCA input files: 1. In the cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ folder, create cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp • Use the exact same configuration as in cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.inp • But replace the geometry line to use cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz instead 2. In the capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ folder, create capped_square_antipris-matic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp • Use the exact same configuration as in capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ.inp • But replace the geometry line to use capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz instead Keep everything else the same as in the original input files, including all computational parameters and settings. input_file_service Action: Tool used: submit_slurm_job Action: Now I'll check the other input file: Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please check the content of capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/-capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp to verify the number of cores and any dependencies. interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ_removed.inp interact_with_os_and_files Action: The file capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_-FREQ_removed.inp specifies the following regarding cores and dependencies: • Number of cores: 16 (set by %pal nprocs 16) • Memory per core: 4000 MB (set by %maxcore 4000) • SCF autotraH: OFF (set by AutotraH false) • Max SCF and geometry optimization iterations: 500 each • Basis set: def2-SVP with def2-ECP for lanthanoids • Functional: PBE0 • Dispersion: D4 • RIJCOSX approximation and DEFGRID2 grid • TightSCF convergence • Geometry input: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz No external dependencies are referenced beyond the specified basis, ECP, and geometry file. The configuration matches the requirements for a 16-core calculation with the correct settings for a lanthanide complex. submit_slurm_job Action: Based on the verification, both input files: 1. Specify 16 cores (%pal nprocs 16) 2. Use the same level of theory and settings (PBE0/def2-SVP with D4 dispersion) 3. Reference distorted XYZ files as input geometries Now I'll submit both jobs in parallel: Tool used: Tool Name: submit_slurm_jobs Arguments: job_names: ['cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ', 'capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ'] number_of_cores: [16, 16] shell_commands: ['orca cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp &gt; cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ_removed.out', 'orca capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp &gt; capped_-square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.out'] submit_slurm_job Action: I've successfully submitted both ORCA jobs in parallel. Both calculations completed successfully with \"ORCA TERMINATED NORMALLY\" status. Here's a summary: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ (using cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp) • Job ID: 230632 • Status: Completed normally • Cores used: 16 2. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ (using capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_-FREQ_removed.inp) • Job ID: 230633 • Status: Completed normally • Cores used: 16 Both jobs used the specified input files that reference distorted geometries as their starting points. The calculations used PBE0/def2-SVP with D4 dispersion, RIJCOSX approximation, and def2-ECP for lanthanoids, as specified in the input files. Output files (including the main .out file, optimized geometries, and trajectory files) have been generated in their respective directories: -cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.out -cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ_removed.xyz -cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed_trj.xyz -capped_square_antipris-matic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.out -capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.xyz -capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed_trj.xyz The jobs ran smoothly without any errors. run_orca Action: I've successfully run both ORCA jobs in parallel. Here's a summary of the results: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ calculation: • Input file: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp • Job ID: 230632 • Status: Completed successfully (\"ORCA TERMINATED NORMALLY\") • Used 16 cores as specified in the input file • Output files generated: -cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.out (main output) -cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.xyz (optimized structure) -cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed_trj.xyz (trajectory file) 2. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ calculation: • Input file: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp • Job ID: 230633 • Status: Completed successfully (\"ORCA TERMINATED NORMALLY\") • Used 16 cores as specified in the input file • Output files generated: -capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.out (main output) -capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.xyz (optimized structure) -capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed_trj.xyz (trajectory file) Both calculations used PBE0/def2-SVP with D4 dispersion, RIJCOSX approximation, and def2-ECP for lanthanoids (Ce) as specified in the input files. Both jobs started from the distorted geometries provided in the respective _distorted.xyz files. perform_dft_calculation Action:</p><p>Now, let me check if the new calculations completed successfully:</p><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please check the output files for the new calculations to determine if they converged successfully: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.out 2. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.out For each calculation, look for \"ORCA TERMINATED NORMALLY\" message and verify that both the geometry optimization and frequency calculations completed successfully. Please report if there are any errors or warnings that need attention.</p><p>interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.out query_keys: ['convergence_information'] interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ_removed.out query_keys: ['convergence_information'] interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.out Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ_removed.out interact_with_os_and_files Action:</p><p>Both output files were checked for convergence and errors:</p><p>1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.out:</p><p>• SCF converged successfully.</p><p>• Geometry optimization completed and converged.</p><p>• No critical errors or warnings, except a note: \"Only C1 symmetry has been detected, increase convergence thresholds\" (not critical for convergence). 2. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_-FREQ_removed.out:</p><p>• SCF converged successfully.</p><p>• Geometry optimization completed and converged.</p><p>• No critical errors or warnings, except a note: \"Only C1 symmetry has been detected, increase convergence thresholds\" (not critical for convergence). Both calculations converged successfully for both geometry optimization and frequency steps. No significant errors or warnings require attention. 1. I read the original input files to ensure I used the exact same configuration:</p><p>• cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.inp • capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ.inp 2. Created new input files with the \"_removed\" suffix while maintaining all computational parameters:</p><p>• cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp • capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ_removed.inp 3. Updated the geometry lines in the new input files to use the distorted geometries:</p><p>• Changed to use cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz • Changed to use capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz 4. Ran both ORCA calculations in parallel using the same settings:</p><p>• PBE0 functional • def2-SVP basis set with def2-ECP for Ce • D4 dispersion correction • RIJCOSX approximation • DEFGRID2 integration grid • 16 cores per calculation 5. Verified that both calculations completed successfully:</p><p>• Both calculations converged for both geometry optimization and frequency parts • Output files were generated with the \"_removed\" suffix as requested • No critical errors or warnings were found The calculations were completed successfully and all specified output files are now available in their respective folders.</p><p>remove_imaginary_frequency Action: Now, let's check if the new calculations still have any imaginary frequencies: Tool used: Tool Name: check_imaginary_frequency Arguments: output_file_path_list: ['cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.out', 'capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_-1_OPT_FREQ_removed.out'] remove_imaginary_frequency Action:</p><p>I notice that both structures still have imaginary frequencies after the first optimization round: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.out: Imaginary frequency of -85.19 cm -¹ in mode 6 2. capped_-square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.out: Imaginary frequency of -14.79 cm -¹ in mode 6 For the capped_square_antiprismatic_1 conformer, the imaginary frequency is now -14.79 cm -¹, which is less than 15 cm -¹ in absolute value. According to our criteria, this can be properly ignored, so we don't need to optimize this structure further. However, the cn9_YICLED conformer still has a significant imaginary frequency of -85.19 cm -¹, so we need to perform another round of optimization for this structure. Let's generate the trajectory file for the remaining structure with significant imaginary frequency:</p><p>Tool used: Tool Name: generate_trajectory_files Arguments: output_file_path_list: ['cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.out'] number_of_mode_list: [6] remove_imaginary_frequency Action: Now let's parse the new distorted xyz coordinates for the cn9_YICLED conformer: Tool used: Tool Name: parse_distored_xyz_image Arguments: xyz_trajectory_file_names: ['cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.out.v006.xyz'] distored_xyz_file_names: ['cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_re-moved_distorted.xyz'] remove_imaginary_frequency Action:</p><p>Now, let's perform a second round of optimization for the cn9_YICLED conformer:</p><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: perform_dft_calculation Arguments: message2agent: Please make sure to read the cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ_removed.inp file and use the exact same configuration for the new calculation, simply replace the xyz file in the geometry line. The new calculation is about performing opt and freq calculation with the cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed_distorted.xyz file. Please name the output file cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.out. Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp interact_with_os_and_files Action: The contents of cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp are: ! OPT FREQ PBE0 def2-SVP D4 RIJCOSX DEFGRID2 TightSCF \\%maxcore 4000 \\%pal nprocs 16 end \\%basis Basis \"def2-SVP\" ECP \"def2-ECP\" end \\%scf AutotraH false MaxIter 500 end \\%geom MaxIter 500 coordsys redundant cartfallback true ReducePrint true end \\%output Print[ P\\_Basis ] 2 Print[ P\\_MOs ] 1 Print[P\\_hirshfeld] 1 end * xyzfile 0 2 cn9\\_YICLED\\_OPT\\_FREQ\\_distorted.xyz perform_dft_calculation Action: Now, I'll create a new input file for the new calculation with the exact same configuration, only changing the geometry file: Tool used: Tool Name: input_file_service Arguments: message2agent: Please create a new ORCA input file in the cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ folder named cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.inp. Use exactly the same configuration as in cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp, but replace the geometry line to use cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed_distorted.xyz instead of cn9_YICLED_-OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz. Keep all other computational parameters exactly the same as in the original input file. input_file_service Action: Tool used: Tool Name: generate_geometry_line Arguments: message2agent: generate the geometry line for cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed_distorted.xyz, the molecule(s) are Ce(III) complex with 3 nitrate_bi and 3 water ligands, the charge and multiplicity are 0 2 generate_geometry_line Action: • xyzfile 0 2 cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed_distorted.xyz input_file_service Action: Tool used: Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity... input_file_service Action:</p><p>The new ORCA input file cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.inp has been created in the cn9_-YICLED_OPT_FREQ folder. It uses exactly the same configuration as cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ_removed.inp, but the geometry line now references cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed_distorted.xyz:</p><p>• Geometry line: * xyzfile 0 2 cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed_distorted.xyz • All other computational parameters and settings are identical to the original input file.</p><p>• The new input file is dependent on cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_-removed_distorted.xyz. The file has been saved as cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.inp. The core specifications are unchanged from the previous input, and the only modification is the geometry file reference.  I'll run the ORCA job as requested. First, let me check the input file to determine the resource requirements and any dependencies:</p><formula xml:id=\"formula_0\">Tool used:</formula><p>Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please check the number of cores required for the ORCA calculation in cn9_-YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.inp and identify any dependent files it may reference. interact_with_os_and_files described above, and each input file is dependent on its respective xyz file.</p><p>perform_dft_calculation Action: Now I'll run the ORCA single point calculations for all five conformers: Tool used: Tool Name: run_orca Arguments: message2agent: Please run the following ORCA jobs in parallel: 1. Run the job in cn9_YICLED_SP folder using the input file cn9_YICLED_SP.inp 2. Run the job in tri_tri_mer_capped_SP folder using the input file tri_tri_mer_capped_SP.inp 3. Run the job in tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP folder using the input file tricapped_trigonal_-prismatic_SP.inp 4. Run the job in capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP folder using the input file capped_square_-antiprismatic_0_SP.inp 5. Run the job in capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP folder using the input file capped_square_-antiprismatic_1_SP.inp run_orca Action: I'll run the five ORCA single point (SP) jobs in parallel. First, let me check the input files to determine resource requirements and dependencies: Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please check the number of cores required for the ORCA calculations in the following input files and identify any dependent files: 1. cn9_YICLED_SP/cn9_YICLED_SP.inp 2. tri_-tri_mer_capped_SP/tri_tri_mer_capped_SP.inp 3. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP/tricapped_-trigonal_prismatic_SP.inp 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP/capped_square_antiprismatic_-0_SP.inp 5. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP.inp interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_SP/cn9_YICLED_SP.inp Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: tri_tri_mer_capped_SP/tri_tri_mer_capped_SP.inp Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP/tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP.inp Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP.inp file are as previously described, with the only change being the removal of \"VV10\" from the keyword line. run_orca Action: Now I'll resubmit the five ORCA single point calculation jobs with the fixed input files: Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: submit_slurm_job Arguments: message2agent: Please submit the following ORCA jobs in parallel with the corrected input files (with VV10 keyword removed): 1. cn9_YICLED_SP folder using the input file cn9_YICLED_SP.inp 2. tri_tri_mer_capped_SP folder using the input file tri_tri_mer_capped_SP.inp 3. tricapped_-trigonal_prismatic_SP folder using the input file tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP.inp 4. capped_-square_antiprismatic_0_SP folder using the input file capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP.inp 5. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP folder using the input file capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-SP.inp Each job requires 16 cores as specified in the input files.</p><p>submit_slurm_job Action:</p><p>I'll submit the ORCA single-point jobs with the corrected input files. First, let me check if the VV10 keyword has been removed from the input files:</p><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please check the content of cn9_YICLED_SP/cn9_YICLED_SP.inp to verify if the VV10 keyword has been removed.</p><p>interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_SP/cn9_YICLED_SP.inp interact_with_os_and_files Action:</p><p>The content of cn9_YICLED_SP/cn9_YICLED_SP.inp does not contain the VV10 keyword. The VV10 keyword has been removed.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>submit_slurm_job</head><p>• Energy check signals convergence. 2. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP.out:</p><p>• SCF converged after 75 cycles.</p><p>• Energy check signals convergence. 3. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP.out:</p><p>• SCF converged after 76 cycles.</p><p>• Gradient check signals convergence. 4. tri_tri_mer_capped_SP/tri_tri_mer_capped_SP.out:</p><p>• SCF converged after 71 cycles.</p><p>• Energy check signals convergence. 5. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP/tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP.out:</p><p>• SCF converged after 68 cycles.</p><p>• Energy check signals convergence. No errors or warnings requiring attention were found in the convergence information for any of the calculations. All calculations terminated normally and SCF cycles converged as expected. </p></div><figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_0\"><head>Figure 3</head><label>3</label><figDesc>Figure 3 Hierarchical architecture of El Agente Q. Inter-agent communication is illustrated with black arrows, where solid arrows represent task delegation from higher-level agents, and dotted arrows indicate feedback and reporting from lower-level agents.The agents can be broadly categorized into three functional modules -the geometry module (coral), the quantum calculation module (blue), and the file I/O module (yellow). Each module can be thought of as a department in a complex organization with its own manager, employees, and tools (violet). For example, the geometry generation agent gets to decide if the system should be handled by the organometallic complexes agent or Perplexity AI search (78), and decide the charge and multiplicity of the system.</figDesc><graphic coords=\"6,82.62,63.78,446.75,408.47\" type=\"bitmap\" /></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_1\"><head>Figure 4 .</head><label>4</label><figDesc>This expansion includes the incorporation of user-friendly interfaces-such as enabling users to draw and manipulate molecular structures directly, similar to the functionality provided by tools like JMol (90; 91), ChemDoodle (92), and ChemDraw (93)-thereby making the system more flexible and accessible to a broader range of academic and industrial researchers (Stage 2). Another key development in the initial stage of El Agente Q will be the expansion of its current toolkit to include spectroscopic property predictions, reaction mechanisms, photochemical processes, and high-level single-and multi-reference electronic structure methods as well as excited state methods, such as DLPNO-CCSD(T) (94), MC-PDFT (95), ADC(2) (96; 97) and NEVPT2 (98) (Stage 3).</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_2\"><head>Figure 4</head><label>4</label><figDesc>Figure 4 El Agente Q Development Roadmap: Bridging Molecular Properties, Advanced Simulations, and Self-Driving Lab Integration. The milestones are not drawn to timescale.</figDesc><graphic coords=\"13,94.38,290.88,423.25,205.16\" type=\"bitmap\" /></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_3\"><head>2 :</head><label>2</label><figDesc>Organize files and perform geometry optimizations</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_4\"><figDesc>Now I'll perform parallel geometry optimizations using ORCA with HF/def2-SVP:Tool used:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_5\"><head>3 :</head><label>3</label><figDesc>Generate reports for each moleculeNow I'll generate individual reports for each molecule by extracting the requested properties from the output files.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_6\"><head>4 :</head><label>4</label><figDesc>Create Comprehensive ReportsNow I'll combine all the extracted information into individual comprehensive reports for each molecule.Tool used:Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity... computational_chemist Action:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_7\"><figDesc>xyz 3. L-phenylalanine_zwitterion/L-phenylalanine_zwitterion_HF_OPT_FREQ/L-phenylalanine_zwitterion_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz 4. 2-chloronitrobenzene/2-chloronitrobenzene_HF_OPT_FREQ/2-chloronitrobenzene_HF_-OPT_FREQ.xyz 5. cis-1_2-cyclohexanediol/cis-1_2-cyclohexanediol_HF_OPT_FREQ/cis-1_2-cyclohexanediol_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz 6. L-histidine_non_zwitterion/L-histidine_non_zwitterion_HF_OPT_FREQ/L-histidine_-non_zwitterion_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz 7. 2_2-biphenol/2_2-biphenol_HF_OPT_FREQ/2_2-biphenol_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz 8. S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol/S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol_HF_OPT_FREQ/S-2-ethyl-2fluoropentan-1-ol_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz 9. R-3-hydroxycyclopentan-1-one/R-3-hydroxycyclopentan-1-one_HF_OPT_FREQ/R-3hydroxycyclopentan-1-one_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz 10. 3-methylbutanoate_anion/3-methylbutanoate_anion_HF_OPT_FREQ/3-methylbutanoate_-anion_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz 11. diisopropylamide_anion/diisopropylamide_anion_HF_OPT_FREQ/diisopropylamide_an-ion_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz 12. diisopropylammonium_cation/diisopropylammonium_cation_HF_OPT_FREQ/diisopropy-lammonium_cation_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz computational_chemist Action:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_8\"><figDesc>ll continue with the process of generating reports for the remaining molecules. Let's proceed with L-phenylalanine_zwitterion: Please extract the following information from the L-phenylalanine_zwitterion/L-phenylalanine_zwitterion_HF_OPT_FREQ/L-phenylalanine_zwitterion_HF_OPT_FREQ.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energy table 6. HOMO-LUMO gap 7. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld charges)Please format the output in markdown format.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_9\"><figDesc>continue generating reports for the remaining molecules, focusing now on 2-chloronitrobenzene: Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please extract the following information from the 2-chloronitrobenzene/2-chloronitrobenzene_HF_OPT_FREQ/2-chloronitrobenzene_HF_OPT_FREQ.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energy table 6. HOMO-LUMO gap 7. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld charges) Please format the output in markdown format.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_10\"><figDesc>continue extracting data and creating reports for the remaining molecules. Let's proceed with cis-1_2-cyclohexanediol: Please extract the following information from the cis-1_2-cyclohexanediol/cis-1_-2-cyclohexanediol_HF_OPT_FREQ/cis-1_2-cyclohexanediol_HF_OPT_FREQ.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energy table 6. HOMO-LUMO gap 7. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld charges)Please format the output in markdown format.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_11\"><figDesc>continue with extracting data and creating reports for the remaining molecules. Let's proceed with L-histidine_non_zwitterion: Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please extract the following information from the L-histidine_non_zwitterion/L-histidine_non_zwitterion_HF_OPT_FREQ/L-histidine_non_zwitterion_HF_OPT_FREQ.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energy table 6. HOMO-LUMO gap 7. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld charges) Please format the output in markdown format.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_12\"><figDesc>Now I'll create the report for L-histidine_non_zwitterion:Tool used:Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity...</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_13\"><figDesc>Please extract the following information from the 2_2-biphenol/2_2-biphenol_-HF_OPT_FREQ/2_2-biphenol_HF_OPT_FREQ.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energy table 6. HOMO-LUMO gap 7. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld charges) Please format the output in markdown format.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_14\"><figDesc>Let's get the charge information from the Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld files for 2_2-biphenol:Tool used:Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please read the contents of these three charge analysis files: 1. 2_2-biphenol/2_-2-biphenol_HF_OPT_FREQ/2_2-biphenol_HF_OPT_FREQ_mulliken_charges.md 2.2_2biphenol/2_2-biphenol_HF_OPT_FREQ/2_2-biphenol_HF_OPT_FREQ_loewdin_charges.md 3. 2_2-biphenol/2_2-biphenol_HF_OPT_FREQ/2_2-biphenol_HF_OPT_FREQ_hirshfeld_charges.md computational_chemist Action:Now let me create the report for 2_2-biphenol:Tool used:Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity...</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_15\"><figDesc>continue extracting data and generating reports for the remaining molecules. Let's extract data for S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol: Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please extract the following information from the S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol/S-2ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol_HF_OPT_FREQ/S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol_HF_OPT_FREQ.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energy table 6. HOMO-LUMO gap 7. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld charges)Please format the output in markdown format.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_16\"><figDesc>to get the charge data for S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol:Tool used:Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please read the contents of these three charge analysis files: 1. S-2-ethyl-2fluoropentan-1-ol/S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol_HF_OPT_FREQ/S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol_-HF_OPT_FREQ_mulliken_charges.md 2. S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol/S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol_HF_OPT_FREQ/S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol_HF_OPT_FREQ_loewdin_charges.md 3.S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol/S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol_HF_OPT_FREQ/S-2-ethyl-2fluoropentan-1-ol_HF_OPT_FREQ_hirshfeld_charges.md computational_chemist Action:Let me create the report for S-2-ethyl-2-fluoropentan-1-ol:Tool used:Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments:code: omitted for brevity...</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_17\"><figDesc>Now I'll create the report for 3-methylbutanoate_anion:Tool used:Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity...</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_18\"><figDesc>Generate the initial geometries for the following molecules using their SMILES representations: 1. Chromium(0) hexacarbonyl (low spin): Cr(=C=O)(=C=O)(=C=O)(=C=O)(=C=O) 2. Chlorine trifluoride: FCl(F)F 3. Fluorophosphoric acid (singly deprotonated form): [O-]P(F)(O)=O computational_chemist Action: Now I'll organize the files into appropriate folders and perform parallel geometry optimizations using ORCA with the specified parameters (HF/def2-SVP in gas phase): folder: Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please create three separate folders for each molecule and move the initial geometry files into their respective folders: 1. Create a folder named \"Cr_hexacarbonyl\" and move chromium_-hexacarbonyl_initial.xyz into it 2. Create a folder named \"ClF3\" and move chlorine_trifluoride_initial.xyz into it 3. Create a folder named \"fluorophosphoric_acid\" and move fluorophosphoric_acid_-deprotonated_initial.xyz into it computational_chemist Action: Now I'll perform the parallel geometry optimizations using ORCA with the HF/def2-SVP method: Tool used:Tool Name: geometry_optimization Arguments: message2agent: Perform parallel geometry optimization for all three molecules using ORCA with the Hartree-Fock (HF) method and def2-SVP basis set in the gas phase. For each molecule:1. Chromium(0) hexacarbonyl (in Cr_hexacarbonyl folder):• Input file: chromium_hexacarbonyl_initial.xyz • Charge: 0 • Multiplicity: 1 (low spin) 2. Chlorine trifluoride (in ClF3 folder):• Input file: chlorine_trifluoride_initial.xyz • Charge: 0 • Multiplicity: 1 3. Fluorophosphoric acid (in fluorophosphoric_acid folder):• Input file: fluorophosphoric_acid_deprotonated_initial.xyz • Charge: -1 • Multiplicity: 1 Use ORCA as the QM software with HF/def2-SVP in the gas phase for all calculations. Please make sure to calculate all properties needed for the report (point group symmetry, dipole moment, molecular orbital analysis, and atomic charge analysis including Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld).</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_19\"><figDesc>Now I'll generate detailed reports for each molecule. I'll start by extracting all the necessary data from the output files:Tool used:Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: For the first molecule (chromium hexacarbonyl), please extract the following information from the Cr_hexacarbonyl/chromium_hexacarbonyl_opt.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energy table 6. HOMO-LUMO gap 7. Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld atomic charges Save each section to separate markdown files in the Cr_hexacarbonyl folder.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_20\"><figDesc>ll create a report for the first molecule (chromium hexacarbonyl):Tool used:Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity...</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_21\"><figDesc>Please visualize the optimized structure of fluorophosphoric acid (deprotonated form) from the file fluorophosphoric_acid/fluorophosphoric_acid_deprotonated_opt.xyz computational_chemist Action: Summary of Completed Tasks I've successfully completed the parallel geometry optimizations of three inorganic compounds using ORCA with the Hartree-Fock method and def2-SVP basis set in the gas phase. Here's a summary of what was accomplished:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_22\"><figDesc>Optimization and AnalysisI'll execute the following steps to complete this request:1. Organize the files by creating folders for each molecule 2. Perform parallel geometry optimization with ORCA using HF/def2-SVP 3. Generate detailed reports for each molecule with the requested informationStep 1: Organizing the filesTool used:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_23\"><head>2 :Step 3 :</head><label>23</label><figDesc>Performing Parallel Geometry OptimizationNow I'll submit all 16 molecules for geometry optimization using ORCA with HF/def2-SVP:Tool used:Tool Name: geometry_optimization Arguments: message2agent: Please optimize the following molecules in parallel using ORCA with the Hartree-Fock (HF) method and def2-SVP basis set in the gas phase. For each molecule, I'm providing the folder name, charge, and multiplicity:1. chromium_hexacarbonyl, charge = 0, multiplicity = 1 2. chlorine_trifluoride, charge = 0, multiplicity = 1 3. fluorophosphoric_acid_singly_deprotonated_form, charge = -1, multiplicity = 1 4. trifluoromethane_sulfonate, charge = -1, multiplicity = 1 5. cyclohexyldimethylphosphine, charge = 0, multiplicity = 1 6. t-butylisothiocyanate, charge = 0, multiplicity = 1 7. chromic_acid, charge = 0, multiplicity = 1 8. permanganic_acid, charge = 0, multiplicity = 1 9. perchlorate, charge = -1, multiplicity = 1 10. hexafluorophosphate, charge = -1, multiplicity = 1 11. tetrafluoroborate, charge = -1, multiplicity = 1 12. dicyanoaurate, charge = -1, multiplicity = 1 13. nitrogen_trifluoride, charge = 0, multiplicity = 1 14. sulfur_hexafluoride, charge = 0, multiplicity = 1 15. sulfur_tetrafluoride, charge = 0, multiplicity = 1 16. xenon_tetrafluoride, charge = 0, multiplicity = 1 computational_chemist Action:Generating Individual ReportsNow I'll generate individual reports for each molecule with all the requested information. I'll create reports one by one, starting with chromium hexacarbonyl: Please extract the following information from the chromium_hexacarbonyl_HF_-OPT_FREQ/chromium_hexacarbonyl.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energies (enough to determine HOMO-LUMO gap) 6. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld) Please visualize the optimized structure of chromium_hexacarbonyl in the chromium_hexacarbonyl_HF_OPT_FREQ folder. Use the final optimized geometry file (not the trajectory file).</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_24\"><figDesc>Now let's continue with the second molecule: chlorine trifluoride. I'll follow the same process.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_25\"><figDesc>Please extract the following information from the chlorine_trifluoride_HF_OPT_-FREQ/chlorine_trifluoride.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energies (enough to determine HOMO-LUMO gap) 6. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld) Please visualize the optimized structure of chlorine_trifluoride in the chlorine_-trifluoride_HF_OPT_FREQ folder. Use the final optimized geometry file. omitted for brevity...</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_26\"><figDesc>continue with the third molecule: fluorophosphoric acid (singly deprotonated form).</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_27\"><figDesc>Please extract the following information from the fluorophosphoric_acid_singly_-deprotonated_form_HF_OPT_FREQ/fluorophosphoric_acid_singly_deprotonated_form.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry (if available) 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energies (enough to determine HOMO-LUMO gap) 6. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld) Please visualize the optimized structure of fluorophosphoric_acid_singly_deproto-nated_form in the fluorophosphoric_acid_singly_deprotonated_form_HF_OPT_FREQ folder. Use the final optimized geometry file.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_28\"><figDesc>omitted for brevity...</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_29\"><figDesc>Let me try to visualize the xenon tetrafluoride using a different approach:Tool used:Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please check the content of the xenon_tetrafluoride_HF_OPT_FREQ/xenon_tetrafluoride.xyz file and fix it if necessary. Then try to visualize the structure again.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_30\"><figDesc>Let me try a different approach to generate an image for xenon tetrafluoride: Tool used: Tool Name: geometry_visualization Arguments: message2agent: Please generate a visualization for the xenon_tetrafluoride molecule from the xenon_tetrafluoride_HF_OPT_FREQ/xenon_tetrafluoride.xyz file. about the electronic structure and properties of each compound. Would you like me to provide any specific additional analysis or comparisons between the molecules? B.4 Relative Stability of Carbocations B.4.1 Level 1</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_31\"><figDesc>Now, let's optimize the carbocations using DFT with B3LYP/6-31G*:Tool used:Tool Name: geometry_optimization code: omitted for brevity...</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_32\"><figDesc>Now I need to organize the generated files and set up for geometry optimization:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_33\"><figDesc>to calculate the reaction energies for cyclo(CnH2n) → cyclo(Cn-1H2n-3)-CH3 reactions. Let's extract the energies from the output files:Tool used:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_34\"><figDesc>Now I'll create a final report based on the post-analysis results:Tool used:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_35\"><head>2 :</head><label>2</label><figDesc>Optimize geometries with DFT using B3LYP/6-31G* and CPCM water solvation Tool used:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_36\"><head>3 :</head><label>3</label><figDesc>Calculate the pKa value using the computational results</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_37\"><head>3 :</head><label>3</label><figDesc>Extract Gibbs free energies for all molecules Tool used:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_38\"><head>Step 4 :</head><label>4</label><figDesc>Calculate ∆G of deprotonation for all acids and calibrate the proton solvation energyLet me calculate ∆G of deprotonation and calibrate the proton solvation energy:Tool used:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_39\"><figDesc>successfully completed the TDDFT calculations for structures 2, 3, and 5 as requested. All calculations were performed using RI-wB2PLYP/def2-mSVP with AutoAux on neutral systems (charge=0, multiplicity=1).</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_40\"><figDesc>completed all the requested tasks. Here's a summary of what was done:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_41\"><head>1 .</head><label>1</label><figDesc>Document computational details in global memory 2. Generate initial geometry for Ce complex with 3 nitrate_bi and 3 water ligands 3. Optimize geometry using PBE0/def2-SVP with specified ORCA settings 4. Perform single point calculations with ωB97M-V/def2-SVPD on optimized geometries 5. Extract and report energies for all conformers Executing Plan First, I'll document the computational details in global memory: Tool used: Tool Name: update_global_memory Arguments: log_content: Lanthanide complex study requirements: -Complex: Ce metal core, +3 oxidation state, CN=9 -Ligands: 3 nitrate_bi and 3 water -Charge: 0, Multiplicity: 2 -Geometry optimization: PBE0/def2-SVP with D4 dispersion, RIJCOSX, DEFGRID2 -Single point: ωB97M-V/def2-SVPD with VV10 dispersion -def2-ECP for lanthanoids -SCF autotraH: OFF for both calculations -Using 16 cores per calculation -Convergence max iterations: 500+ for both opt and SCF -TightSCF or extreme tight settings required computational_chemist Action:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_42\"><figDesc>generated the initial geometries for the Ce complex according to the specifications. Since Ce is a lanthanide metal (element 58), I used the specialized metal complex geometry generation tool. The system generated 5 different conformers of the complex with the following characteristics: -Metal center: Ce (Cerium) -Oxidation state: +3 -Coordination number: 9 -Ligands: 3 nitrate_bi ligands and 3 water ligands -Complex charge: 0 (neutral) -Multiplicity: 2 The generated conformers represent different possible arrangements of the ligands around the Ce(III) center: 1. cn9_YICLED_0_nunpairedes_0_charge_0.xyz 2. tri_tri_mer_capped_0_nunpairedes_-0_charge_0.xyz 3. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_1_nunpairedes_0_charge_0.xyz 4. capped_-square_antiprismatic_0_nunpairedes_0_charge_0.xyz 5. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_nun-pairedes_0_charge_0.xyz Let me update the global memory with this information:Tool used:Tool Name: update_global_memory Arguments: log_content: Ce complex system profile: -Metal: Ce(III) -Coordination number: 9 -Ligands: 3 nitrate_bi + 3 water -Charge: 0 -Multiplicity: 2 -Conformers generated: 5 (cn9_YICLED, tri_tri_mer_capped, tricapped_trigonal_prismatic, and two capped_square_antiprismatic variants)</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_43\"><figDesc>custom ORCA %basis block for a Ce(III) complex. -All atoms use def2-SVP basis set. -Ce uses def2-ECP effective core potential. Optimized %basis block: %basis Basis \"def2-SVP\" ECP \"def2-ECP\" end This block ensures all atoms use def2-SVP, and Ce uses the def2-ECP as required. No unnecessary auxiliary or decontraction options were included. Configuration_Recommender Action: PBE0 def2-SVP global_memory_expert • 16 cores (%pal nprocs 16) • Global memory: %maxcore 4000 • Output block included as per best practice • Charge: 0, Multiplicity: 2 (geometry line references the correct xyz file for each conformer) The input files and their dependencies are: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ.inp (depends on cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_0_nunpairedes_0_charge_-0_xtb.xyz) 2. tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ/tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ.inp (depends on tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ/tri_tri_mer_capped_0_nunpairedes_0_charge_0_xtb.xyz) 3. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ/tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_-FREQ.inp (depends on tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ/tricapped_trigonal_pris-matic_1_nunpairedes_0_charge_0_xtb.xyz) 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ/-capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ.inp (depends on capped_square_antiprismatic_0_-OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_nunpairedes_0_charge_0_xtb.xyz) 5. capped_-square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ.inp (depends on capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_nunpairedes_-0_charge_0_xtb.xyz) All files have been stored in the correct directories. The core specifications for each input file are as described above, and each input file is dependent on its respective xyz file. perform_dft_calculation Action: Now I'll run the ORCA calculations for all five conformers: Tool used:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_44\"><figDesc>The ORCA input file cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.inp produced an error: 'Unknown identifier in SCF block line 13: Last token: TIGHTSCF.' The SCF block used was: %scf AutotraH false MaxIter 500 TightSCF true end . Please provide a corrected SCF block for a Ce(III) complex with SCF autotraH OFF, max iterations 500, and TightSCF convergence criteria.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_45\"><figDesc>Here is the corrected SCF block for your ORCA input: %scf AutotraH false MaxIter 500 ConvCriteria Tight end This uses ConvCriteria Tight, which is the correct keyword for tight SCF convergence in ORCA.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_46\"><figDesc>generate the geometry line for cn9_YICLED_0_nunpairedes_0_charge_0_xtb.xyz, the molecule(s) are Ce(III) complex with 3 nitrate_bi and 3 water ligands, the charge and multiplicity are</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_47\"><figDesc>omitted for brevity...</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_48\"><figDesc>Now I'll resubmit the fixed jobs in parallel:Tool used:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_49\"><figDesc>Now I'll check if all the calculations completed successfully:Tool used:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_50\"><head>3 :</head><label>3</label><figDesc>Check for and Remove Imaginary FrequenciesNow I'll check for and remove any imaginary frequencies that might be present in the DFT optimized structures:Tool used:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_51\"><figDesc>Completed additional geometry optimization with frequency calculations for two distorted Ce complex conformers (cn9_YICLED and capped_square_antiprismatic_1) using ORCA with the same PBE0/def2-SVP/D4/RIJCOSX settings as the original calculations. Both calculations converged successfully. perform_dft_calculation Action: I've successfully completed the additional geometry optimization and frequency calculations for the distorted Ce complex conformers as requested. Here's a summary of what was done:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_52\"><figDesc>create a new calculation based on the exact configuration from the cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp file, changing only the geometry file as requested. Let me first read the existing input file:Please read the cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp file and show its contents.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_53\"><figDesc>Now, I'll run the new ORCA calculation with the created input file:Tool used:Tool Name: run_orca Arguments: message2agent: Please run the ORCA job in the cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ folder using the input file cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.inp. The output file should be named cn9_YICLED_-OPT_FREQ_removed2.out.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_54\"><head>2 .</head><label>2</label><figDesc>Completed single point calculations for all five Ce complex conformers using optimized geometries with ωB97M-V functional and def2-SVPD basis set (def2-ECP for Ce). The VV10 nonlocal dispersion keyword was incompatible with the installed ORCA version, so calculations were performed without it. All calculations converged successfully. perform_dft_calculation Action: I have successfully completed single point calculations for all five Ce complex conformers using their optimized geometries. Here's a summary of what was done:1. Created job folders for each conformer with the \"_SP\" suffix:Copied the optimized geometry files to their respective SP job folders: • cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.xyz to cn9_YICLED_SP/ • tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ.xyz to tri_tri_mer_capped_SP/ • tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ.xyz to tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP/ • capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ.xyz to capped_square_antiprismatic_0_-SP/ • capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.xyz to capped_square_antipris-matic_1_SP/ 3. Created ORCA input files for single point calculations with the specified settings: • Functional: ωB97M-V • Basis set: def2-SVPD with def2-ECP for Ce • SCF autotraH: OFF • TightSCF convergence criteria</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_55\"><graphic coords=\"3,82.62,63.78,446.76,259.09\" type=\"bitmap\" /></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_56\"><graphic coords=\"4,94.38,446.20,423.24,172.71\" type=\"bitmap\" /></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_57\"><graphic coords=\"46,134.95,70.87,262.95,199.01\" type=\"bitmap\" /></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_58\"><graphic coords=\"46,401.20,74.18,262.94,195.69\" type=\"bitmap\" /></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_59\"><graphic coords=\"135,70.87,63.78,470.27,335.91\" type=\"bitmap\" /></figure>\n\t\t\t<note xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" place=\"foot\" n=\"1\" xml:id=\"foot_0\"><p>Pronounced /el a'xente/, approximately \"el ah-HEN-teh\" in English. See audio: Zenodo (MP3).</p></note>\n\t\t\t<note xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" place=\"foot\" n=\"2\" xml:id=\"foot_1\"><p>This field was left empty due to an API bug; overall performance was unaffected.</p></note>\n\t\t</body>\n\t\t<back>\n\n\t\t\t<div type=\"acknowledgement\">\n<div><head>Acknowledgements</head><p>We thank <rs type=\"person\">Alastair Price</rs> and <rs type=\"person\">Sergio Pablo Garcia</rs> for their helpful discussions, which provided valuable insights during the development of this work. We are also grateful to <rs type=\"person\">Maria Luiza Linazzi</rs> and <rs type=\"person\">Prof. David Armstrong</rs> for generously sharing the CHM 323 class exercises at the <rs type=\"institution\">University of Toronto</rs>, which inspired the benchmark used in this paper. We acknowledge <rs type=\"person\">Alex Zhavoronkov</rs> and <rs type=\"person\">Insilico Medicine</rs> for open-sourcing the design element library named <rs type=\"person\">Icons Pack</rs>, which was used to create some of the figures in this article. This work incorporates elements licensed under the <ref type=\"url\" target=\"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/\">Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 4.0  International (CC BY-NC-SA)</ref> license. Y.Z. and Z.Z acknowledge support from <rs type=\"funder\">NSERC -IRCPJ 547644</rs>. A.H.C., C.T.S., G.T., A.W. and <rs type=\"person\">C.Cr</rs>. acknowledge the generous support of the <rs type=\"funder\">Canada 150 Research Chairs Program</rs>. A.A. acknowledges support from <rs type=\"funder\">King Abdullah University of Science and Technology (KAUST)</rs> for the <rs type=\"grantName\">KAUST Ibn Rushd Postdoctoral Fellowship</rs>. J.B. acknowledges support from the <rs type=\"funder\">ONR</rs> grant with award # <rs type=\"grantNumber\">N00014-21-1-2137</rs>. S.X.L. acknowledges support from <rs type=\"institution\">Nanyang Technological University, Singapore</rs> and the <rs type=\"institution\">Ministry of Education, Singapore</rs> for the Overseas Postdoctoral Fellowship. C.Ch. acknowledges support from the <rs type=\"funder\">DOE</rs> grant with the <rs type=\"funder\">University of Minnesota</rs> award # <rs type=\"grantNumber\">A006801504</rs>. G.T. acknowledges support from <rs type=\"funder\">NSERC</rs> through the <rs type=\"grantName\">Postgraduate Scholarship-Doctoral program</rs> # <rs type=\"grantNumber\">PSGD3-559078-2021</rs>, and the <rs type=\"funder\">Vector Institute</rs>. J.A.C.-G.-A. acknowledges support from the <rs type=\"funder\">NSERC</rs> alliance grant # <rs type=\"grantNumber\">ALLRP 587593-23</rs>. A.W. acknowledges support from the <rs type=\"funder\">NSERC Canada Graduate Scholarships -Doctoral Program</rs>. A.A.-G. thanks <rs type=\"person\">Anders G. Frøseth</rs> for his generous support. A.A.-G. also acknowledges the generous support of <rs type=\"funder\">Natural Resources Canada</rs> and the <rs type=\"funder\">Canada 150 Research Chairs program</rs>. This research is part of the <rs type=\"institution\">University of Toronto's Acceleration Consortium</rs>, which receives funding from the <rs type=\"funder\">Canada First Research Excellence Fund (CFREF)</rs> via <rs type=\"grantNumber\">CFREF-2022-00042</rs>. A.A.-G. thanks the <rs type=\"institution\">NVIDIA</rs> research and product development teams for useful discussions.</p></div>\n\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t<listOrg type=\"funding\">\n\t\t\t\t<org type=\"funded-project\" xml:id=\"_DeckQ6H\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<orgName type=\"project\" subtype=\"full\">Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 4.0 International (CC BY-NC-SA</orgName>\n\t\t\t\t</org>\n\t\t\t\t<org type=\"funding\" xml:id=\"_C7hvKMm\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<orgName type=\"grant-name\">KAUST Ibn Rushd Postdoctoral Fellowship</orgName>\n\t\t\t\t</org>\n\t\t\t\t<org type=\"funding\" xml:id=\"_ebGvTAK\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<idno type=\"grant-number\">N00014-21-1-2137</idno>\n\t\t\t\t</org>\n\t\t\t\t<org type=\"funding\" xml:id=\"_bMAzxMj\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<idno type=\"grant-number\">A006801504</idno>\n\t\t\t\t</org>\n\t\t\t\t<org type=\"funding\" xml:id=\"_mgHcGkU\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<idno type=\"grant-number\">PSGD3-559078-2021</idno>\n\t\t\t\t\t<orgName type=\"grant-name\">Postgraduate Scholarship-Doctoral program</orgName>\n\t\t\t\t</org>\n\t\t\t\t<org type=\"funding\" xml:id=\"_S3HeQU6\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<idno type=\"grant-number\">ALLRP 587593-23</idno>\n\t\t\t\t</org>\n\t\t\t\t<org type=\"funding\" xml:id=\"_6jzDjr6\">\n\t\t\t\t\t<idno type=\"grant-number\">CFREF-2022-00042</idno>\n\t\t\t\t</org>\n\t\t\t</listOrg>\n\n\t\t\t<div type=\"availability\">\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Data and code availability</head><p>All the data required to evaluate the presented conclusions are available via <ref type=\"url\" target=\"https://doi.org/10.5683/SP3/JU2BQK\">https://doi.org/10.5683/SP3/  JU2BQK</ref>.</p></div>\n\t\t\t</div>\n\n\t\t\t<div type=\"annex\">\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Arguments:</head><p>message2agent: Please visualize the optimized structure of triazaadamantane from the file: triazaadamantane/triazaadamantane_HF_OPT_FREQ/triazaadamantane_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz computational_chemist Action:</p><p>Let me create the report for triazaadamantane:</p><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments:</p><p>code: omitted for brevity...</p><p>computational_chemist Action:</p><p>I'll now continue to generate reports for all the remaining molecules. Let's generate reports for the remaining 15 molecules systematically by implementing a Python script that will handle this process efficiently:</p><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity...</p><p>computational_chemist Action:</p><p>Now I'll proceed to create visualizations for all the remaining molecules, and then extract the data for their reports. Let's start with visualizing limonene:</p><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: geometry_visualization Arguments: message2agent: Please visualize the optimized structure of limonene from the file: limonene/limonene_HF_OPT_FREQ/limonene_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz computational_chemist Action: Now I'll continue with the process by creating visualizations for the remaining molecules, extracting data from the output files, and generating reports. Let me visualize the D-glucose structure next: computational_chemist Action: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: For the second molecule (chlorine trifluoride), please extract the following information from the ClF3/chlorine_trifluoride_opt_2.out file (which contains the final optimized structure with no imaginary frequencies): 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energy table 6. HOMO-LUMO gap 7. Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld atomic charges Save each section to separate markdown files in the ClF3 folder.</p><p>computational_chemist Action:</p><p>Let's create the report for chlorine trifluoride:</p><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity...</p><p>computational_chemist Action:</p><p>Now, let's visualize the optimized structure of chlorine trifluoride:</p><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: geometry_visualization Arguments: message2agent: Please visualize the optimized structure of chlorine trifluoride from the file ClF3/chlorine_trifluoride_opt_2.xyz computational_chemist Action:</p><p>Finally, let's extract the data for the third molecule (fluorophosphoric acid):</p><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: For the third molecule (fluorophosphoric acid, deprotonated form), please extract the following information from the fluorophosphoric_acid/fluorophosphoric_acid_deprotonated_opt.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energy table 6. HOMO-LUMO gap 7. Mulliken, Action: I'll continue generating reports for the remaining molecules. The next one to process is tetrafluoroborate. computational_chemist Action: Tool used: computational_chemist Action: computational_chemist Action:</p><p>Now I'll continue with the reports for the remaining molecules. Let's process dicyanoaurate next.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head n=\"12.\">Dicyanoaurate Report</head><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please extract the following information from the dicyanoaurate_HF_OPT_-FREQ/dicyanoaurate.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry (if available) 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energies (enough to determine HOMO-LUMO gap) 6. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld)</p><p>computational_chemist Action: Tool used: computational_chemist Action: computational_chemist Action:  computational_chemist Action: computational_chemist Action:     Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please generate a simple representation of the xenon tetrafluoride structure from the coordinates in the output file using a text-based approach or any other method that works in the current environment. Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity... computational_chemist Action: Tool used: Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity... computational_chemist Action: Summary of Completed Tasks I have successfully completed the following tasks as requested: 1. Geometry Optimization: Performed parallel geometry optimization for all 16 inorganic compounds using ORCA with the Hartree-Fock (HF) method and def2-SVP basis set in the gas phase. The calculations included frequency analysis to confirm that each structure represents a true energy minimum. 2. Report Generation: Created detailed individual reports for each molecule containing: • Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) • Total energy (in Hartrees) • Point group symmetry • Dipole moment (in Debye) • Molecular orbital analysis (including HOMO-LUMO gap) • Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld)</p><p>• Visualization of optimized structures (except for xenon tetrafluoride, where a description was provided instead) 3. Organization: All reports have been saved in a dedicated 'reports' directory, with a summary report providing an overview of all calculations. All 16 molecules were successfully optimized to energy minima at the HF/def2-SVP level, as confirmed by frequency analysis showing no imaginary frequencies. The reports contain comprehensive information Arguments: message2agent: Optimize the carbocations (methyl+, ethyl+, propyl+, 2-methylpropyl+, touluene+, benzene+, dimethyl ether+, trimethylamine+, and propene+) using DFT with B3LYP functional and 6-31G* basis set. The carbocations have charge 1 and multiplicity 1. Use the XYZ files in the carbocations folder as input.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>computational_chemist Action:</head><p>Let's run the DFT calculation for the hydride:</p><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: perform_dft_calculation Arguments: message2agent: Perform a DFT calculation for the hydride ion (H-) using B3LYP/6-31G* basis set. The hydride has charge -1 and multiplicity 1. Use the XYZ file in the hydride folder as input. Include frequency calculations to get thermodynamic data. Now that all the calculations are complete, let's extract the relevant thermodynamic data from the output files to calculate the carbocation formation enthalpies and Gibbs free energies:</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Tool used:</head><p>Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Extract the enthalpy and Gibbs free energy values from the output files for all the optimized molecules (neutral molecules, carbocations, and hydride). For the propyl cation and 2methylpropyl cation, use the data from the final optimized structures that have no imaginary frequencies (the _attempt1 files).</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>computational_chemist Action:</head><p>Let's extract the data for the propyl cation from its output file:</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Tool used:</head><p>Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Extract the enthalpy and Gibbs free energy values from the C3H7_plus_opt_-freq_attempt1.out file in the C3H7_plus_opt_freq folder. Now that we have all the necessary data, let's calculate the carbocation formation enthalpies and Gibbs free energies and create a summary table:</p><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: Arguments: message2agent: Generate the geometry for the hydride ion (H-) with charge = -1 and multiplicity = 1. This is a single hydrogen atom with a negative charge.  Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please extract the electronic energy, enthalpy, and Gibbs free energy values from all the optimized molecule output files (.out files in the _optfreq directories). For the hydride ion, extract the values from the hydride_sp/hydride.out file. For molecules that went through imaginary frequency removal (propene, ethylcation, propylcation), use the values from the _distorted.out files. The values should be extracted in hartrees.  Here's a summary of the work performed:</p><p>1. Generated initial geometries for: • 9 R-H molecules (methane, ethane, propane, 2-methylpropane, toluene, benzene, dimethyl ether, trimethylamine, and propene) • 9 R+ carbocations • 1 hydride ion (H-) 2. Optimized all geometries using DFT with B3LYP/6-31G* basis set:</p><p>• For R-H molecules: all neutral compounds with multiplicity 1 • For R+ cations: all with +1 charge and multiplicity 1 • For H-: with -1 charge and multiplicity 1 • Resolved imaginary frequencies for propene, ethylcation, and propylcation 3. Calculated carbocation formation enthalpies and Gibbs free energies for the reaction: R-H → R+ + H-4. Created a comprehensive results table that has been saved to 'carbocation_formation_energies.md'</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Key Results</head><p>The carbocation formation energies (in kcal/mol) are:</p><p>Ring Strain Energies (RSE) Reference: cyclooctane (n = 8) n Cycloalkane RSE (Eh) RSE (kcal/mol) RSE (kJ/mol) 3 cyclopropane 0.00946385 5.939 24.847 4 cyclobutane 0.00908831 5.703 23.861 5 cyclopentane -0.01613549 -10.125 -42.364 6 cyclohexane -0.02309439 -14.492 -60.634 7 cycloheptane -0.00778332 -4.884 -20.435 8 cyclooctane 0.00000000 0.000 0.000 Action:</p><p>I need to save the report from the post_analysis_expert to a file:</p><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please create a file called \"report.md\" with the following content:</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Ring Strain Analysis of Cycloalkanes via Reaction Energies Introduction</head><p>Ring strain is a major factor governing the stability and reactivity of cycloalkanes. In this study, we estimate relative ring strain energies of cyclopropane through cyclooctane by computing reaction enthalpies (∆H) and free energies (∆G) for the formal transformation cyclo-(C n H 2n ) → methylcyclo-(C n-1 H 2n-3 ) using B3LYP/6-31G(d) enthalpies and free energies. Cyclohexane is taken as a zero-strain reference.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Methodology</head><p>• Energies (enthalpy H and Gibbs free energy G) in hartree were obtained from DFT frequency calculations at B3LYP/6-31G(d).</p><p>• Reaction energies ∆H and ∆G (hartree) converted to kcal/mol by multiplying with 1 hartree = 627.5095 kcal/mol.</p><p>• Reaction ∆(n) = H(methylcyclo (n-1) ) -H(cyclo n ) (and similarly for G) approximates the difference in ring strain between the n-membered ring and the (n-1)-membered ring.</p><p>• Cyclohexane (n=6) strain is set to zero. Ring strain for n&lt;6 accumulated downward via strain(n-1) = ∆H(n) + strain(n) and for n&gt;6 upward via strain(n) = strain(n-1) -∆H(n). Let me organize the report files into a dedicated folder:</p><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please create a folder named \"results\" and move the report.md file and ring_strain_-vs_size.png (if it exists) into that folder. Let me also create a simple visualization of the optimized geometries to include with our report:</p><formula xml:id=\"formula_1\">Tool used:</formula><p>Tool Name: geometry_visualization Arguments: message2agent: Please create visualizations of all the optimized geometries for the cycloalkanes (cyclopropane to cyclooctane) and display them side by side in order from smallest to largest ring size. Use ball-and-stick representation and clearly show the ring structure. The optimized geometries should be found in their respective optimization folders. Save this visualization to the results folder. Tool Name: update_global_memory Arguments: log_content: Completed calculation of ring strain energies for cycloalkanes (n=3-8) using B3LYP/6-31G(d) level of theory. Analyzed reaction energies for cyclo(CnH2n) → cyclo(Cn-1H2n-3)-CH3 transformations (n=4-8) and used them to estimate relative ring strain. Established cyclohexane as zero-strain reference. Found greatest strain in cyclopropane (13.86 kcal/mol) and cyclobutane (13.63 kcal/mol), negative strain for cyclopentane (-2.20 kcal/mol), and moderate strain in cycloheptane (8.13 kcal/mol) and cyclooctane (10.85 kcal/mol). Created full report with data tables, molecule visualizations, and analysis in the results folder.</p><p>computational_chemist Action: Summary of Ring Strain Energy Calculations I've successfully completed the calculation of ring strain energies for cycloalkanes (n=3-8) using B3LYP/6-31G(d) level of theory. Here's a summary of what was accomplished: 1. Generated geometries for all required molecules: • Cycloalkanes: cyclopropane through cyclooctane • Methylcycloalkanes: methylcyclopropane through methylcycloheptane 2. Optimized all geometries at B3LYP/6-31G(d) level with frequency calculations to obtain thermodynamic data • Resolved an imaginary frequency issue with cyclobutane by distorting along the vibrational mode and re-optimizing 3. Calculated reaction energies for the conversions: Action: Step 4: Visualize the optimized structures Tool used: Tool Name: geometry_visualization Arguments: message2agent: Please visualize both the optimized structures of acetic acid and acetate ion. Use the files acetic_acid_opt_freq/acetic_acid_opt_freq.xyz and acetate_ion_opt_freq/acetate_ion_-opt_freq.xyz. computational_chemist Action: Summary of pKa Calculation for Acetic Acid</p><p>I've successfully completed the calculation of the pKa for acetic acid in water using B3LYP/6-31G* with CPCM implicit solvation model. Here's a summary of the process and results:</p><p>1. Generated initial geometries for both acetic acid (CH3COOH) and its conjugate base, acetate ion (CH3COO-) 2. Optimized both structures using B3LYP/6-31G* with CPCM water solvation, including frequency calculations 3. Extracted Gibbs free energies from the optimization calculations:</p><p>• Acetic acid: -228.93401544 Hartree (-143658.16 kcal/mol)</p><p>• Acetate ion: -228.46232720 Hartree (-143362.17 kcal/mol) 4. Calculated the pKa using the equation: pKa = ∆G/(2.303*RT)</p><p>• Deprotonation free energy: 30.09 kcal/mol • Calculated pKa: 22.05 The calculated pKa of 22.05 is higher than the experimental value (approximately 4.76), which is common in computational pKa predictions and may be due to: -Limitations of the DFT method and basis set -Simplifications in the solvation model -Need for thermodynamic cycle corrections A detailed report has been saved as \"acetic_acid_pka_report.md\" and the optimized molecular structures have been visualized.</p><p>B.6.2 Level 2 Query: Plan and act directly. Do not ask for my confirmation this time. Complete the following request: Calculate the pKa of chlorofluoroacetic acid using B3LYP/6-31G*. To do so, first calibrate the free energy of solvation of the proton based on the known literature values of some related carboxylic acids: 1. Acetic acid; pKa = 4.76 2. Fluoroacetic acid; pKa = 2.586 3. Chloroacetic acid; pKa = 2.86 folder: pKaOfCarboxylicAcidsAndMethods_level2_6 computational_chemist Action: pKa Calculation for Chlorofluoroacetic Acid I'll calculate the pKa of chlorofluoroacetic acid using B3LYP/6-31G* and calibrate the free energy of solvation of the proton based on known reference acids. Step 1: Generate geometries for all required molecules Tool used: Tool Name: geometry_generation Arguments: message2agent: Generate the following molecules: 1. Acetic acid (CH3COOH) 2. Acetate ion (CH3COO-) 3. Fluoroacetic acid (CH2FCOOH) 4. Fluoroacetate ion (CH2FCOO-) 5. Chloroacetic acid (CH2ClCOOH) 6. Chloroacetate ion (CH2ClCOO-) 7. Chlorofluoroacetic acid (CHClFCOOH) 8. Chlorofluoroacetate ion (CHClFCOO-) 9. Proton (H+) Please create these geometries in separate files with appropriate names. computational_chemist Action: Step 2: Optimize all geometries with B3LYP/6-31G* and implicit solvation</p><p>Let me organize the files and then optimize all geometries:</p><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Create separate directories for each molecule and move the corresponding files to these directories: 1. Create a directory \"acetic_acid\" and move acetic_acid_initial.xyz to it 2. Create a directory \"acetate_ion\" and move acetate_ion_initial.xyz to it 3. Create a directory \"fluoroacetic_acid\" and move fluoroacetic_acid_initial.xyz to it 4. Create a directory \"fluoroacetate_ion\" and move fluoroacetate_ion_initial.xyz to it 5. Create a directory \"chloroacetic_acid\" and move chloroacetic_-acid_initial.xyz to it 6. Create a directory \"chloroacetate_ion\" and move chloroacetate_ion_initial.xyz to it 7. Create a directory \"chlorofluoroacetic_acid\" and move chlorofluoroacetic_acid_initial.xyz to it 8. Create a directory \"chlorofluoroacetate_ion\" and move chlorofluoroacetate_ion_initial.xyz to it 9. Create a directory \"proton\" and move proton_initial.xyz to it  Now that I've organized the files, I'll examine the XYZ files to determine the multiplicity of each molecule:</p><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please read the contents of 2.xyz, 3.xyz, and 5.xyz to check their structures and determine their multiplicities. Since all the molecules are neutral as specified in the request, we know the charge is 0. For organic molecules with an even number of electrons, the multiplicity is typically 1 (singlet) in the ground state. Let's proceed with the TDDFT calculations for all three molecules:  Let me check the input file we used to make sure we properly included triplet states calculation:</p><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please read the file tddft_2/tddft_2.inp to check the TDDFT settings we used.</p><p>computational_chemist Action:</p><p>I need to modify the TDDFT input to explicitly include triplet states. Let's update the input for the remaining molecules:</p><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: perform_dft_calculation Arguments: message2agent: Perform a single-point TDDFT calculation for molecule 2.xyz with RI-wB2PLYP/def2-mSVP and AutoAux. The molecule is neutral with charge 0 and multiplicity 1. Include both singlet and triplet excited states by explicitly adding 'TripletFlag true' in the TDDFT block. Calculate at least 5 roots for both singlet and triplet states. The job should be named 'tddft_2_updated'.  The calculations provided all the requested information: 1. S1 energy levels for each molecule 2. T1 energy levels for each molecule 3. S1-T1 energy differences 4. Oscillator strengths to the S1 state For the first molecule, I had to update the input to explicitly include triplet states calculation using the 'Triplets true' keyword in the TDDFT block. This was applied to all subsequent calculations to ensure consistent methodology across all molecules. The report has been saved as \"tddft_results_summary.md\" for future reference. three times stronger than Structure 3's oscillator strength. The calculations were completed successfully, and all requested data has been extracted from the TDDFT output files. A detailed report has been generated as \"tddft_summary_report.md\" in the working directory.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>C Supplemental note 2: Case Studies</head><p>In this section, we present the queries and agent responses for the two selected case studies, highlighting their initial and final stages, as well as selected intermediate points to illustrate the agent's behaviour across different tasks.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>C.1 Influence of Solvent on IR Spectra</head><p>Query: Investigate how adding implicit or explicit solvent molecules(water) changes the frequencies of vibrations for an Alanine molecule Compare IR spectrum of calculation from gas phase, within the solvent (CPCM) and within the solvent with the actual solvent molecule (add 12 H2O around the Alanine molecule +CPCM). Use PBE0 def2-TZVP for all 3 systems. Make a report at the end.</p><p>Note that in earlier versions of the system, a dedicated geometry module agent was introduced to mediate interactions among the geometry generation, optimization, and visualization agents. This design has since been simplified, with these components now directly connected.</p><p>folder: IRSpec computational_chemist Action: Investigating Solvent Effects on Alanine Vibrational Frequencies I'll help you investigate how different solvent models affect the vibrational frequencies of alanine. This requires three DFT calculations with frequency analysis: 1. Gas phase (no solvent) 2. Implicit solvent (CPCM water) 3. Explicit solvent (12 H2O molecules around Alanine + CPCM) All using PBE0/def2-TZVP as specified. Initial Plan: 1. Generate optimized geometry for alanine 2. Create models for all three solvent conditions 3. Perform frequency calculations on all systems 4. Analyze and compare IR spectra 5. Generate a comprehensive report Should I proceed with generating the optimized geometry for alanine first? 1. Generate and optimize alanine molecule in gas phase with frequency calculation 2. Generate and optimize alanine molecule with implicit solvent (CPCM water) with frequency calculation 3. Generate and optimize alanine with 12 explicit water molecules + CPCM with frequency calculation 4. Extract IR spectra from all three systems Let me proceed with creating and optimizing these systems.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Tool used:</head><p>Tool Name: solve_geometry_tasks Arguments: message2agent: Please create and optimize the following three systems with frequency calculations using PBE0/def2-TZVP level of theory:</p><p>1. Alanine in gas phase (filename: alanine_gas) 2. Alanine with CPCM implicit water solvent (filename: alanine_cpcm) 3. Alanine with 12 explicit water molecules plus CPCM water (filename: alanine_explicit) For all systems, perform both geometry optimization and frequency calculation in one job. Name the output files accordingly.</p><p>solve_geometry_tasks Action:    Now that I have generated all three geometries, I'll proceed with the optimization and frequency sponding IR intensities 2. From alanine_cpcm_opt_freq/alanine_cpcm.out, extract all vibrational frequencies and their corresponding IR intensities 3. From alanine_explicit_opt_freq/alanine_explicit.out, extract all vibrational frequencies and their corresponding IR intensities Please provide the data in a structured format that can be easily processed for analysis and comparison.</p><p>computational_chemist Action:</p><p>Let me also extract the IR intensities to properly generate a comparative analysis of the vibrational spectra:</p><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: For each of the three alanine systems, extract the IR spectral data that includes both frequencies and their corresponding IR intensities from the output files. For the explicit solvent system, please extract just the first 50 vibrational modes if the file is too large. Specifically:</p><p>1. From alanine_gas_opt_freq/alanine_gas_ir.md, extract the frequency and IR intensity data 2. From alanine_cpcm_opt_freq/alanine_cpcm_ir.md, extract the frequency and IR intensity data 3. From alanine_explicit_opt_freq/alanine_explicit_ir.md, extract the frequency and IR intensity data Please provide the data in a format that shows the frequency (cmˆ{}-1) and IR intensity for each mode. Great! I've successfully completed the investigation of solvent effects on alanine vibrational frequencies. Let me organize the remaining files and provide you with a summary of the findings:</p><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please move the following files to the alanine_spectrum_analysis folder if they exist in the root directory: 1. alanine_ir_comparison.png 2. alanine_solvation_report.md</p><p>Then, provide a list of all files in the alanine_spectrum_analysis folder to verify everything is properly organized.</p><p>computational_chemist Action: %MaxCore 4000 generate_geometry_line Action: • xyzfile 0 2 capped_square_antiprismatic_1_nunpairedes_0_charge_0_xtb.xyz geom_block_expert Action:</p><p>%geom MaxIter 500 coordsys redundant cartfallback true ReducePrint true end Task completed: Generated a minimal %geom block for a geometry optimization of a Ce(III) complex with a user-specified maximum of 500 iterations. No constraints, scans, or special settings were requested. All other parameters are set to recommended defaults for robust optimization.</p><p>pal_block_expert Action:</p><p>Task Completed: Configured the ORCA %pal block for a Ce(III) complex input to use 16 cores for parallelization, as requested. The block is as follows: %pal nprocs 16 end This configuration efficiently utilizes the available resources on the Mariana cluster (which has 48 cores), staying within the recommended range (not exceeding 24 cores). No advanced nprocs_group setting was specified, as the user did not indicate a need for numerical gradients or frequencies. No errors encountered.  run_orca Action:</p><p>Great! I've verified that all the required dependencies are already in their respective job folders. Each input file specifies 16 cores for the calculation and has its corresponding .xyz file in the same directory. Now, I'll submit all five ORCA jobs in parallel:</p><p>Tool used: Tool Name: submit_slurm_job Arguments: message2agent: Please submit the following ORCA jobs in parallel: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ (input file: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.inp) 2. tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ (input file: tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ.inp) 3. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ (input file: tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ.inp) 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ (input file: capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ.inp) 5. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ (input file: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ.inp) Each job requires 16 cores as specified in their input files. submit_slurm_job Action:</p><p>I'll submit the five ORCA jobs in parallel, but first let me verify the settings in each input file to confirm the core count and check for any dependencies.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Tool used:</head><p>Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please check the content of cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ.inp to verify the number of cores and any dependencies.</p><p>interact_with_os_and_files Action: submit_slurm_job Action:</p><p>Based on the verification, all five input files are properly configured with 16 cores (%pal nprocs 16) and have the appropriate xyz files in their respective directories. I'll now submit all five jobs in parallel:</p><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: submit_slurm_jobs Arguments: job_names: ['cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ', 'tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ', 'tricapped_trig-onal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ', 'capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ', 'capped_square_-antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ'] number_of_cores: <ref type=\"bibr\">[16,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\">16,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\">16,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\">16,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\">16]</ref> shell_commands: ['orca cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.inp &gt; cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.out', 'orca tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ.inp &gt; tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ.out', 'orca tri-capped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ.inp &gt; tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ.out', 'orca capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ.inp &gt; capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_-FREQ.out', 'orca capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ.inp &gt; capped_square_antipris-matic_1_OPT_FREQ.out']</p><p>submit_slurm_job Action: Great! All the input files have been corrected by removing the \"ConvCriteria\" parameter from the SCF blocks. The files are now showing just \"AutotraH false\" and \"MaxIter 500\" in the SCF block, which should prevent the previous errors. Let me now submit all five jobs in parallel using 16 cores for each job:</p><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: submit_slurm_jobs Arguments: job_names: ['cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ', 'tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ', 'tricapped_trig-onal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ', 'capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ', 'capped_square_-antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ'] number_of_cores: <ref type=\"bibr\">[16,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\">16,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\">16,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\">16,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\">16]</ref> shell_commands: ['orca cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.inp &gt; cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.out', 'orca tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ.inp &gt; tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ.out', 'orca tri-capped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ.inp &gt; tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ.out', 'orca capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ.inp &gt; capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_-FREQ.out', 'orca capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ.inp &gt; capped_square_antipris-matic_1_OPT_FREQ.out'] All five ORCA jobs have been successfully completed. Here's a summary of what was done:</p><p>1. Initial preparation:</p><p>• Verified that each job folder contained the required input file and dependent .xyz file • Confirmed that all input files specified 16 cores for the calculation 2. First submission attempt:</p><p>Tool Name: generate_geometry_line Arguments: message2agent: generate the geometry line for cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz, the molecule(s) are Ce(III) complex with 3 nitrate_bi and 3 water ligands, the charge and multiplicity are 0 2 Tool Name: generate_geometry_line Arguments: message2agent: generate the geometry line for capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz, the molecule(s) are Ce(III) complex with 3 nitrate_bi and 3 water ligands, the charge and multiplicity are 0 2 generate_geometry_line Action: • xyzfile 0 2 cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz • xyzfile 0 2 capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz input_file_service Action: Tool used: Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity... input_file_service Action: The following new ORCA input files have been created as requested: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp • Configuration: Identical to cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.inp • Geometry line updated to: * xyzfile 0 2 cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz • Depends on: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz 2. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_-FREQ_removed.inp • Configuration: Identical to capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ.inp • Geometry line updated to: * xyzfile 0 2 capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz • Depends on: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antipris-matic_1_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz All other computational parameters and settings remain unchanged from the original input files. The new input files have been saved in their respective directories and are ready for use. The core specifications are as previously described, and each input file is dependent on its respective distorted xyz file. perform_dft_calculation Action: Now I'll run the ORCA calculations using the new input files with the distorted geometries: Tool used: Tool Name: run_orca Arguments: message2agent: Please run the following ORCA jobs in parallel: 1. Run the job in cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ folder using the input file cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ_removed.inp 2. Run the job in capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ folder using the input file capped_-square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp run_orca Action: I'll run the two ORCA jobs in parallel as requested. First, let me check the input files to determine the resource requirements and any dependencies: Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please check the number of cores required for the ORCA calculations in the following input files and identify any dependent files: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ_removed.inp 2. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antipris-matic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ_removed.inp interact_with_os_and_files Action: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp: • Number of cores required: 16 (specified by nprocs 16 in the %pal block) • Dependent file: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz (referenced in the * xyzfile line) 2. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_-FREQ_removed.inp: • Number of cores required: 16 (specified by nprocs 16 in the %pal block) • Dependent file: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz (referenced in the * xyzfile line) Both input files require 16 cores and depend on their respective distorted.xyz coordinate files. run_orca Action:</p><p>I can see that both input files require 16 cores and depend on their respective _distorted.xyz files, which are already present in their directories. Now I'll submit the two ORCA jobs in parallel:</p><p>Tool used: Tool Name: submit_slurm_job Arguments: message2agent: Please submit the following ORCA jobs in parallel: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ folder using the input file cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp 2. capped_square_-antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ folder using the input file capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_-FREQ_removed.inp Each job requires 16 cores as specified in the input files. submit_slurm_job Action: I'll submit the two ORCA jobs in parallel. First, let me check the input files to verify the core count and any dependencies: Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please check the content of cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ_removed.inp to verify the number of cores and any dependencies. interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp interact_with_os_and_files Action: The file cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp specifies the following regarding cores and dependencies: • Number of cores: 16 (set by the block %pal nprocs 16 end) • Memory per core: 4000 MB (set by %maxcore 4000) • No explicit external dependencies are referenced, but the input uses: -PBE0 functional -def2-SVP basis set and def2-ECP for lanthanoids -D4 dispersion correction -RIJCOSX approximation -DEFGRID2 grid -TightSCF convergence -Max 500 iterations for both geometry and SCF -Input geometry is read from cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_distorted.xyz No other external files or dependencies are referenced in the input file. Action: Tool used: Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.inp interact_with_os_and_files Action: The ORCA input file cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.inp specifies the following: • Number of cores required: 16 (set by the block %pal nprocs 16 end) • Dependent file referenced: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed_distorted.xyz (referenced in the * xyzfile 0 2 . . . line) Summary: -Cores: 16 -Dependent file: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_-removed_distorted.xyz run_orca Action: Now I'll submit the ORCA job as requested: Tool used: Tool Name: submit_slurm_job Arguments: message2agent: Please submit the ORCA job in the cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ folder using the input file cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.inp. The job requires 16 cores as specified in the input file. Make sure the output file will be named cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.out. submit_slurm_job Action: I'll submit the requested ORCA job. First, let me check the input file to verify the core count and any dependencies: Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please check the content of cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ_removed2.inp to verify the number of cores and any dependencies. interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: read_file_content Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.inp interact_with_os_and_files Action: The file cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.inp specifies the following regarding cores and dependencies: • Number of cores: 16 (set by the block %pal nprocs 16 end) • Memory per core: 4000 MB (set by %maxcore 4000) • No explicit external dependencies are referenced in the input file. All required basis sets and ECPs are defined within the file. • The calculation uses the following methods and settings: OPT FREQ PBE0 def2-SVP D4 RIJCOSX DEFGRID2 TightSCF, with additional settings for SCF, geometry, and output printing. Summary: 16 cores are requested, and all dependencies (basis, ECP, etc.) are defined internally in the input file. No external dependencies are referenced. submit_slurm_job Action: Now I'll submit the requested ORCA job using the specified input file: Tool used: Tool Name: submit_slurm_jobs Arguments: job_names: ['cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ'] number_of_cores: [16] shell_commands: ['orca cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.inp &gt; cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ_removed2.out'] -cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.out (main output file as requested) -cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.xyz (optimized geometry) -cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2_trj.xyz (trajectory file) -cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2_atom58.out (additional output) The calculation used the PBE0 functional with def2-SVP basis set, D4 dispersion correction, RIJCOSX approximation, and def2-ECP for the lanthanoid metal center (Ce) as specified in the input file. The job started from the geometry provided in the cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed_distorted.xyz file. All output files are now available in the cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ directory.</p><p>perform_dft_calculation Action:</p><p>Now let me check if the calculation completed successfully:</p><formula xml:id=\"formula_2\">Tool used:</formula><p>Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please check the cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_re-moved2.out file to determine if the calculation converged successfully. Look for \"ORCA TERMINATED NORMALLY\" message and verify that both the geometry optimization and frequency calculations completed successfully. Please report if there are any errors or warnings that need attention.</p><p>interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used:  The calculation in cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.out converged successfully:</p><p>• The SCF (Self-Consistent Field) procedure converged after 5 cycles.</p><p>• The geometry optimization reached convergence, as indicated by \"*** THE OPTIMIZATION HAS CONVERGED ***\".</p><p>• There is no indication of errors or failed steps in the convergence information.</p><p>• There is a note: \"Only C1 symmetry has been detected, increase convergence thresholds\", but this is informational and not an error or warning that affects convergence. No errors or critical warnings were found that require attention. Both the geometry optimization and frequency calculations completed successfully.</p><p>perform_dft_calculation Action: Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: update_global_memory Arguments: log_content: Completed an additional geometry optimization with frequency calculation for a second distorted Ce complex conformer (cn9_YICLED) using ORCA with the exact same PBE0/def2-SVP settings as the previous calculations. The calculation used cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed_distorted.xyz as input and generated cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.out as output. The calculation converged successfully. perform_dft_calculation Action:</p><p>I've successfully completed the requested calculation for the additional distorted Ce complex conformer. Here's a summary of what was done:</p><p>1. I first read the existing input file cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed.inp to ensure I used the exact same configuration and settings. 2. Created a new input file cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.inp with:</p><p>• The exact same ORCA configuration: PBE0 functional, def2-SVP basis set, D4 dispersion, RIJCOSX approximation, etc.</p><p>• Changed only the geometry line to reference the new XYZ file: cn9_YICLED_OPT_-FREQ_removed_distorted.xyz 3. Run the ORCA calculation with the new input file, which generated:</p><p>• cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.out (main output file as requested) • cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.xyz (optimized geometry)</p><p>• Additional output files including the trajectory file 4. Verified that the calculation completed successfully:</p><p>• SCF converged properly • Geometry optimization converged successfully • Frequency calculation completed without errors • No critical warnings were found in the output The calculation was completed successfully, and all output files are now available in the cn9_YICLED_-OPT_FREQ directory with the specified naming convention.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>remove_imaginary_frequency Action:</head><p>Let's check if the newest calculation still has any imaginary frequencies:</p><p>Tool used: Tool Name: check_imaginary_frequency Arguments: output_file_path_list: ['cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_re-moved2.out'] remove_imaginary_frequency Action:</p><p>Great news! The final cn9_YICLED structure (cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.out) now has no imaginary frequencies.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Summary of Imaginary Frequency Removal</head><p>I've successfully checked and removed imaginary frequencies from all five Ce complex conformers. Here are the detailed results:</p><p>1. Initial check results:</p><p>• Three conformers had no imaginary frequencies:</p><p>-tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ -tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ -capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ • Two conformers had imaginary frequencies:</p><p>-cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ: -131.99 cm -¹ (mode 6) -capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ: -81.23 cm -¹ (mode 6) 2. First optimization round:</p><p>• Generated distorted structures along the imaginary normal modes • After optimization, the conformers showed:</p><p>-cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed: -85.19 cm -¹ (still significant) -capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed: -14.79 cm -¹ (below our 15 cm -¹ threshold, can be ignored) 3. Second optimization round (only for cn9_YICLED):</p><p>• Generated another distorted structure for cn9_YICLED • After optimization, cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2 showed no imaginary frequencies All five Ce complex conformers now have either no imaginary frequencies or only negligible ones (below 15 cm -¹ in absolute value). The optimized structures represent true local minima on the potential energy surface and are suitable for further analysis.</p><p>geometry_optimization Action: Geometry Optimization Report for Ce Complex Conformers</p><p>I have successfully completed the geometry optimization of all five Ce complex conformers according to your specifications. Here's a detailed report of the process and final results:</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Summary of Optimization Process</head><p>1. XTB Optimization: All five conformers were initially optimized using XTB. 2. DFT Optimization: The XTB-optimized structures were then subjected to DFT optimization with frequency calculations using ORCA with PBE0/def2-SVP/D4/RIJCOSX settings. 3. Imaginary Frequency Removal: Three conformers had no imaginary frequencies after DFT optimization, while two conformers required additional optimization steps to remove imaginary frequencies.</p></div>\n<div xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\"><head>Final Optimized Geometries</head><p>The most optimized geometry files for each conformer are:</p><p>1. cn9_YICLED: cn9\\_YICLED\\_OPT\\_FREQ\\_removed2.xyz • Required two rounds of imaginary frequency removal • Initial imaginary frequency: -131.99 cm -¹ • After first removal: -85.19 cm -¹ • Final structure: No imaginary frequencies 2. tri_tri_mer_capped: tri\\_tri\\_mer\\_capped\\_OPT\\_FREQ.xyz • No imaginary frequencies after initial DFT optimization 3. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic: tricapped\\_trigonal\\_prismatic\\_OPT\\_FREQ.xyz • No imaginary frequencies after initial DFT optimization 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_0: capped\\_square\\_antiprismatic\\_0\\_OPT\\_FREQ.xyz • No imaginary frequencies after initial DFT optimization 5. capped_square_antiprismatic_1: capped\\_square\\_antiprismatic\\_1\\_OPT\\_FREQ\\_removed.xyz • Initial imaginary frequency: -81.23 cm -¹ • After removal: -14.79 cm -¹ (below the 15 cm -¹ threshold, considered negligible) All five conformers now represent true local minima on the potential energy surface with either no imaginary frequencies or only negligible ones (below 15 cm -¹ in absolute value). These optimized structures are suitable for further analysis or calculations. computational_chemist Action: Now I'll proceed with the single point calculations using the optimized geometries with the specified settings: Tool used: Tool Name: perform_dft_calculation Arguments: message2agent: Perform single point calculations for all five Ce complex conformers using their optimized geometries. The optimized geometries are located in: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.xyz 2. tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ/tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ.xyz 3. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ/tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ.xyz 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_-FREQ.xyz 5. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_-FREQ_removed.xyz Use ORCA with the following settings: -Functional: ωB97M-V (wB97M-V) -Basis set: def2-SVPD with def2-ECP for Ce -VV10 nonlocal dispersion correction -SCF autotraH: OFF -TightSCF convergence criteria -Cores: 16 -Max iterations: 500</p><p>The complex has charge 0 and multiplicity 2 for all conformers. Please name the output directory for each conformer with \"_SP\" after the conformer name, e.g., \"cn9_YICLED_SP\". Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please create 5 job folders with the following names: 1. cn9_YICLED_SP 2. tri_-tri_mer_capped_SP 3. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP 5. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP Then copy the respective optimized geometry files into each folder: 1. Copy cn9_YICLED_-OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.xyz to cn9_YICLED_SP/ 2. Copy tri_-tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ/tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ.xyz to tri_tri_mer_capped_-SP/ 3. Copy tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ/tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_-FREQ.xyz to tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP/ 4. Copy capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_-FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ.xyz to capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP/ 5. Copy capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_-FREQ_removed.xyz to capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP/ interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity... interact_with_os_and_files Action: All 5 job folders were created as requested: 1. cn9_YICLED_SP 2. tri_tri_mer_capped_SP 3. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP 5. capped_square_-antiprismatic_1_SP The respective optimized geometry files were copied into each folder: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FRE-Q/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.xyz → cn9_YICLED_SP/ 2. tri_tri_mer_capped_-OPT_FREQ/tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ.xyz → tri_tri_mer_capped_SP/ 3. tricapped_-trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ/tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ.xyz → tricapped_-trigonal_prismatic_SP/ 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antipris-matic_0_OPT_FREQ.xyz → capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP/ 5. capped_square_antipris-matic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.xyz → capped_-square_antiprismatic_1_SP/ Task completed successfully. perform_dft_calculation Action: Now I'll create the ORCA input files for single point calculations with the specified settings: Tool used: Tool Name: input_file_service Arguments: message2agent: Please create ORCA input files for single point calculations for all 5 conformers with the following specifications: 1. For folder cn9_YICLED_SP: • XYZ file: cn9