 all of them. Tool Name: submit_slurm_jobs Arguments: job_names: ['cn9_YICLED_SP', 'tri_tri_mer_capped_SP', 'tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP', 'capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP', 'capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP'] number_of_cores: <ref type=\"bibr\">[16,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\">16,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\">16,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\">16,</ref><ref type=\"bibr\">16]</ref> shell_commands: ['orca cn9_YICLED_SP.inp &gt; cn9_YICLED_SP.out', 'orca tri_tri_mer_-capped_SP.inp &gt; tri_tri_mer_capped_SP.out', 'orca tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP.inp &gt; tr-icapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP.out', 'orca capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP.inp &gt; capped_-square_antiprismatic_0_SP.out', 'orca capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP.inp &gt; capped_square_-antiprismatic_1_SP.out']  All five ORCA single point calculations have been successfully completed. Here's a summary of the work that was done:</p><p>1. Initial Assessment:</p><p>• I checked all five input files and found they require 16 cores each • Each SP calculation depends on its corresponding optimized geometry (.xyz file)</p><p>• All dependencies were already in place in their respective directories 2. Initial Submission and Error:</p><p>•  Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Please check the output files for all five single point calculations to determine if they converged successfully. For each calculation, check the corresponding .out file in the respective folder and look for \"ORCA TERMINATED NORMALLY\" message and verify that the SCF converged successfully. Please report if there are any errors or warnings that need attention. Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_SP/cn9_YICLED_SP.out query_keys: ['convergence_information'] interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP.out query_keys: ['convergence_information'] Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP.out query_keys: ['convergence_information'] Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: tri_tri_mer_capped_SP/tri_tri_mer_capped_SP.out query_keys: ['convergence_information'] Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP/tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP.out query_keys: ['convergence_information'] interact_with_os_and_files Action:</p><p>All five single point calculations converged successfully. Here are the details for each .out file: 1. cn9_YICLED_SP/cn9_YICLED_SP.out:</p><p>• SCF converged after 60 cycles.</p><p>• 16 cores per calculation • Max iterations: 500 • Charge: 0, Multiplicity: 2 4. During execution, I encountered an issue: the VV10 nonlocal dispersion keyword was not compatible with the installed ORCA version. To proceed with the calculations, I removed this keyword while keeping all other settings unchanged. 5. Successfully ran all five calculations in parallel using ORCA. 6. Verified the results:</p><p>• All five calculations converged successfully • SCF convergence was achieved in 60-76 cycles depending on the conformer • No critical errors or warnings were found • All calculations terminated normally All single point calculations for the five Ce complex conformers were completed successfully, and the output files are available in their respective job folders with the requested naming convention. Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Extract the final energies from both the optimization and single point calculation output files for all conformers. For each conformer, please extract:</p><p>1. From the optimization output files (the final optimization if there were multiple steps):</p><p>• Final energy (Eh) from PBE0/def2-SVP calculation 2. From the single point output files:</p><p>• Final energy (Eh) from ωB97M-V/def2-SVPD calculation The files to extract from are: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_-removed2.out and cn9_YICLED_SP/cn9_YICLED_SP.out 2.</p><p>tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_-FREQ/tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ.out and tri_tri_mer_capped_SP/tri_tri_mer_-capped_SP.out 3. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ/tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_-OPT_FREQ.out and tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP/tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP.out 4.</p><p>capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_-FREQ.out and capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP.out 5. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ_removed.out and capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP.out</p><note type=\"other\">interact_with_os_and_files</note><p>Action: Tool used: Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.out query_keys: ['TOTAL SCF ENERGY'] Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: cn9_YICLED_SP/cn9_YICLED_SP.out query_keys: ['TOTAL SCF ENERGY'] interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ/tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ.out query_keys: ['TOTAL SCF ENERGY'] Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: tri_tri_mer_capped_SP/tri_tri_mer_capped_SP.out query_keys: ['TOTAL SCF ENERGY'] interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ/tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_-FREQ.out query_keys: ['TOTAL SCF ENERGY'] Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP/tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP.out query_keys: ['TOTAL SCF ENERGY'] interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_-OPT_FREQ.out query_keys: ['TOTAL SCF ENERGY'] Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP.out query_keys: ['TOTAL SCF ENERGY'] interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_-OPT_FREQ_removed.out query_keys: ['TOTAL SCF ENERGY'] Tool Name: extract_properties_from_orca_outputfile Arguments: file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP.out query_keys: ['TOTAL SCF ENERGY'] interact_with_os_and_files Action: Here are the extracted final energies (in Hartree, Eh) for each conformer from the specified ORCA output files: 1. cn9_YICLED • Optimization (PBE0/def2-SVP): -1543.61225634143420 Eh • Single Point (ωB97M-V/def2-SVPD): -1544.53545294825108 Eh 2. tri_tri_mer_capped • Optimization (PBE0/def2-SVP): -1543.61465542569977 Eh • Single Point (ωB97M-V/def2-SVPD): -1544.53675364646824 Eh 3. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic • Optimization (PBE0/def2-SVP): -1543.61397373074919 Eh • Single Point (ωB97M-V/def2-SVPD): -1544.53704995156204 Eh 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_0 • Optimization (PBE0/def2-SVP): -1543.61290222139519 Eh • Single Point (ωB97M-V/def2-SVPD): -1544.53720655504048 Eh 5. capped_square_antiprismatic_1 • Optimization (PBE0/def2-SVP): -1543.60826276741545 Eh • Single Point (ωB97M-V/def2-SVPD): -1544.53307801377878 Eh All values are the final SCF energies from the respective calculations. Task completed successfully. computational_chemist Action: Let's create a final report with the extracted energies: Tool used: Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity... computational_chemist Action:</p><p>Let's now visualize one of the optimized geometries to see the structure of the Ce complex:</p><p>Tool used:</p><p>Tool Name: geometry_visualization Arguments: message2agent: Please visualize the optimized geometry of the most stable Ce complex conformer, which is capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ.xyz in the capped_square_antiprismatic_-0_OPT_FREQ directory. Show the structure with a ball-and-stick representation with Ce atom highlighted.</p><p>geometry_visualization Action: Tool used: Tool Name: visualize_xyz Arguments: xyz_file_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_-0_OPT_FREQ.xyz output_image_path: capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antipris-matic_0_OPT_FREQ_ball_and_stick.png geometry_visualization Action:</p><p>The optimized geometry of the most stable Ce complex conformer (capped_square_antiprismatic_0_-OPT_FREQ.xyz) has been visualized in a ball-and-stick representation. The Ce atom is highlighted in the generated image. You can view the structure in the following file: capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ_-ball_and_stick.png Let me know if you need the image displayed or require further analysis. Let's create a proper directory to store our final report and organize everything:</p><formula xml:id=\"formula_3\">Tool used:</formula><p>Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Create a directory called \"Final_Report\" and move the ce_complex_analysis.md file into it. Also, copy the visualization image from capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ/-capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ_ball_and_stick.png to the Final_Report directory as \"most_stable_conformer.png\".</p><p>interact_with_os_and_files Action: Tool used: Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity... interact_with_os_and_files Action:</p><p>The following tasks have been completed: -Created a directory called \"Final_Report\". -Moved the ce_complex_analysis.md file into the \"Final_Report\" directory.</p><p>-Copied the visualization image from capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_-FREQ_ball_and_stick.png to the \"Final_Report\" directory and renamed it as \"most_stable_conformer.png\". computational_chemist capped_-square_an-tiprismatic_1 -1543.60826276741545 -1544.53307801377878 Relative Energies (in kcal/mol) Conformer PBE0/def2-SVP Relative ωB97M-V/def2-SVPD Relative capped_square_antiprismatic_0 1.10 0.00 (most stable) tricapped_trigonal_prismatic 0.43 0.10 tri_tri_mer_capped 0.00 (most stable) 0.28 cn9_YICLED 1.51 1.10 capped_square_antiprismatic_1 4.01 2.59 Note that the relative energies are calculated with respect to the most stable conformer for each level of theory: -At the PBE0/def2-SVP optimization level, tri_tri_mer_capped is the most stable conformer -At the ωB97M-V/def2-SVPD single point level, capped_square_antiprismatic_0 is the most stable conformer</p><p>The conformers are ordered by increasing relative energy at the more accurate ωB97M-V/def2-SVPD level, which shows that the energy differences between the most stable three conformers are relatively small (&lt; 0.3 kcal/mol).</p></div><figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_62\"><figDesc>Please visualize the optimized structure of D-glucose from the file: D-glucose/D-glucose_HF_OPT_FREQ/D-glucose_HF_OPT_FREQ.xyz computational_chemist Arguments: message2agent: Please visualize the optimized structure of chromium hexacarbonyl from the file Cr_hexacarbonyl/chromium_hexacarbonyl_opt.xyz computational_chemist Action: Now let's extract the data for the second molecule (chlorine trifluoride):Tool used:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_64\"><figDesc>Please extract the following information from the tetrafluoroborate_HF_OPT_-FREQ/tetrafluoroborate.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry (if available) 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energies (enough to determine HOMO-LUMO gap) 6. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld) Please visualize the optimized structure of tetrafluoroborate in the tetrafluorobo-rate_HF_OPT_FREQ folder. Use the final optimized geometry file.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_65\"><figDesc>Please visualize the optimized structure of dicyanoaurate in the dicyanoaurate_-HF_OPT_FREQ folder. Use the final optimized geometry file. ll continue with the report for the 13th molecule: nitrogen trifluoride.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_66\"><figDesc>Please extract the following information from the nitrogen_trifluoride_HF_OPT_-FREQ/nitrogen_trifluoride.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry (if available) 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energies (enough to determine HOMO-LUMO gap) 6. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld) Please visualize the optimized structure of nitrogen_trifluoride in the nitrogen_-trifluoride_HF_OPT_FREQ folder. Use the final optimized geometry file.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_67\"><figDesc>omitted for brevity...</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_68\"><figDesc>Now I'll continue generating reports for the remaining molecules. Let's work on sulfur hexafluoride.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_69\"><figDesc>Please extract the following information from the sulfur_hexafluoride_HF_OPT_-FREQ/sulfur_hexafluoride.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry (if available) 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energies (enough to determine HOMO-LUMO gap) 6. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld) Please visualize the optimized structure of sulfur_hexafluoride in the sulfur_-hexafluoride_HF_OPT_FREQ folder. Use the final optimized geometry file. omitted for brevity...</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_70\"><figDesc>Now let's continue with the report for the 15th molecule: sulfur tetrafluoride.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_71\"><figDesc>Please extract the following information from the sulfur_tetrafluoride_HF_OPT_-FREQ/sulfur_tetrafluoride.out file: 1. Final Cartesian coordinates (in Å) 2. Total energy (in Hartrees) 3. Point group symmetry (if available) 4. Dipole moment (in Debye) 5. Molecular orbital energies (enough to determine HOMO-LUMO gap) 6. Atomic charge analysis (Mulliken, Löwdin, and Hirshfeld) Please visualize the optimized structure of sulfur_tetrafluoride in the sulfur_-tetrafluoride_HF_OPT_FREQ folder. Use the final optimized geometry file.Let me try to retrieve and visualize the xenon tetrafluoride molecule a different way:Tool used:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_75\"><figDesc>Now let's organize our files into appropriate folders and then optimize all the geometries using DFT with B3LYP/6-31G*: Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Create directories for each molecule type and move the initial geometries into their respective folders: 1. Create a directory called \"RH_molecules\" 2. Create a directory called \"Rplus_cations\" 3. Create a directory called \"hydride\" 4. Move all R-H molecule files (_initial.xyz) except hydride_initial.xyz and cation files to the RH_molecules directory 5. Move all carbocation files ( cation_initial.xyz) to the Rplus_cations directory 6. Move hydride_initial.xyz to the hydride directory computational_chemist Action: Now let's optimize all the R-H molecules using DFT with B3LYP/6-31G*: Tool used: Tool Name: geometry_optimization Arguments: message2agent: Optimize the geometry of all R-H molecules in the RH_molecules directory using DFT with B3LYP functional and 6-31G* basis set. All molecules have charge 0 and multiplicity 1. Please perform the optimization for: 1. methane_initial.xyz 2. ethane_initial.xyz 3. propane_initial.xyz 4. methylpropane_initial.xyz 5. toluene_initial.xyz 6. benzene_initial.xyz 7. dimethylether_initial.xyz 8. trimethylamine_initial.xyz 9. propene_initial.xyz computational_chemist Action: Now let's optimize all the carbocations (R+) using DFT with B3LYP/6-31G*: Optimize the geometry of all carbocation molecules in the Rplus_cations directory using DFT with B3LYP functional and 6-31G* basis set. All molecules have charge 1 and multiplicity 1. Please perform the optimization for: 1. methylcation_initial.xyz 2. ethylcation_initial.xyz 3. propylcation_initial.xyz 4. methylpropylcation_initial.xyz 5. tolylcation_initial.xyz 6. phenyl-cation_initial.xyz 7. methoxymethylcation_initial.xyz 8. trimethylammoniumcation_initial.xyz 9. propenylcation_initial.xyz computational_chemist Action: Now let's optimize the hydride ion (H-) using DFT with B3LYP/6-31G*: Tool used: Tool Name: geometry_optimization Arguments: message2agent: Optimize the geometry of the hydride ion (H-) in the hydride directory using DFT with B3LYP functional and 6-31G* basis set. The molecule has charge -1 and multiplicity 1. Please perform the optimization for hydride_initial.xyz.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_76\"><figDesc>Now let's extract the energy information from all the output files to calculate the carbocation formation enthalpies and Gibbs free energies:Tool used:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_77\"><figDesc>Now let's calculate the carbocation formation enthalpies and Gibbs free energies and save the results to a text file:Tool used:Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity...</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_78\"><figDesc>Let's visualize some of the optimized structures to verify our calculations: Please visualize the following pairs of molecules to compare their structures: 1. Methane (methane_optfreq/methane.xyz) and the Methyl cation (methylcation_optfreq/methylcation.xyz) 2. Benzene (benzene_optfreq/benzene.xyz) and the Phenyl cation (phenylcation_optfreq/phenylcation.xyz) 3. Propene (propene_optfreq/propene_distorted.xyz) and the Propenyl cation (propenylcation_optfreq/propenylcation.xyz) computational_chemist Action: Summary of Carbocation Formation Energy Calculations I've successfully completed the carbocation formation energy calculations for the specified molecules.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_79\"><head>Results 1 .</head><label>1</label><figDesc>Reaction Energies (∆H, ∆G in kcal/mol) Action:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_82\"><figDesc>Compute the energy level of S1, the energy difference between S1 and T1, and the oscillator strength to the S1 state for the following structures: 2.xyz, 3.xyz, and 5.xyz. Perform a single-point TDDFT calculation with RI-wB2PLYP/def2-mSVP and AutoAux in the keyword line. Hint: You need to turn on the excited triplet states calculation. The oscillator strength values correspond to fosc(P2) in the output.Calculations and AnalysisI'll organize and execute TDDFT calculations for the three molecules to obtain the requested properties:1. Organize the XYZ files into separate folders 2. Perform single-point TDDFT calculations with RI-wB2PLYP/def2-mSVP including triplet states 3. Extract the requested properties from output files 4. Compile results in a summary report Let me start by organizing the files:Tool used:Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Create folders for each molecule (2, 3, and 5) and move the corresponding XYZ files into them.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_85\"><figDesc>Perform a single-point TDDFT calculation for molecule 2.xyz with RI-wB2PLYP/def2-mSVP and AutoAux. Include both singlet and triplet excited states. The molecule is neutral with charge 0 and multiplicity 1. We need to compute S1 energy level, T1 energy level, and oscillator strength. The job should be named 'tddft_2'.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_87\"><figDesc>Now that I have the correct setup for the TDDFT calculations, let's run the remaining calculations for molecules 3 and 5:Tool used:Tool Name: perform_dft_calculation Arguments:message2agent: Perform a single-point TDDFT calculation for molecule 3.xyz with RI-wB2PLYP/def2-mSVP and AutoAux. The molecule is neutral with charge 0 and multiplicity 1. Include both singlet and triplet excited states by explicitly adding 'Triplets true' in the TDDFT block. Calculate at least 5 roots for both singlet and triplet states. The job should be named 'tddft_3'.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_88\"><figDesc>Now let's run the TDDFT calculation for molecule 5:Tool used:Tool Name: perform_dft_calculation Arguments:message2agent: Perform a single-point TDDFT calculation for molecule 5.xyz with RI-wB2PLYP/def2-mSVP and AutoAux. The molecule is neutral with charge 0 and multiplicity 1. Include both singlet and triplet excited states by explicitly adding 'Triplets true' in the TDDFT block.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_89\"><head>B. 7 . 2 2 Query:</head><label>722</label><figDesc>LevelCompute the energy level of S1, the energy difference between S1 and T1, and the oscillator strength to the S1 state for the following structures: 2.xyz, 3.xyz, and 5.xyz. Perform a single-point TDDFT calculation with RI-wB2PLYP/def2-mSVP and AutoAux in the keyword line.Calculations on Multiple StructuresI'll organize the files and perform TDDFT calculations to determine the S1 energy level, S1-T1 energy difference, and oscillator strength for structures 2, 3, and 5. Steps to execute: 1. Organize the xyz files into separate folders 2. Set up and perform TDDFT single-point calculations for all structures in parallel 3. Extract the relevant data from output files 4. Analyze and compile the results Let me begin with the implementation: Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Create directories for each molecule: mkdir -p 2 3 5; then move the xyz files into their respective directories: mv 2.xyz 2/; mv 3.xyz 3/; mv 5.xyz 5/; finally list all directories to confirm: ls -la computational_chemist Action: Now let's check the contents of the XYZ files to understand the molecules we're working with: Tool used: Tool Name: interact_with_os_and_files Arguments: message2agent: Check the content of each XYZ file to understand the molecule structure. Please read 2/2.xyz, 3/3.xyz, and 5/5.xyz and report the number of atoms and general structure of each molecule. computational_chemist Action: Now I'll set up and perform the TDDFT calculations for all three structures in parallel. According to the requirements, I need to: -Use RI-wB2PLYP/def2-mSVP with AutoAux -Calculate for neutral systems -Get S1 energy level, S1-T1 difference, and oscillator strength Tool used: Tool Name: perform_dft_calculation Arguments: message2agent: Please perform single-point TDDFT calculations for structures 2, 3, and 5. Use the coordinates from 2/2.xyz, 3/3.xyz, and 5/5.xyz. The calculation details are as follows: -Method: Key Observations: 1. Structure 3 has the lowest S1 energy level (1.698 eV), while Structure 5 has the highest (3.196 eV). 2. Structure 3 has the smallest S1-T1 energy gap (0.256 eV), while Structure 2 has the largest (0.348 eV). 3. Structure 5 demonstrates the strongest oscillator strength to S1 (0.077525), which is more than</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_90\"><figDesc>Solvent Effects on Alanine Vibrational FrequenciesI'll generate and optimize all three systems with frequency calculations using PBE0/def2-TZVP, then extract the IR spectra for analysis. Here's my proposed approach:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_91\"><figDesc>help you create and optimize the three alanine systems with frequency calculations using PBE0/def2-TZVP. Let's do this step by step. First, I'll generate the initial geometries for all three systems:Tool used:Tool Name: geometry_generation Arguments: message2agent: Please create the geometry for alanine molecule in gas phase. Save it as alanine_gas.xyz.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_92\"><figDesc>Now I'll create the alanine with CPCM implicit water solvent:Tool used:Tool Name: geometry_generation Arguments: message2agent: Please create the geometry for alanine molecule with CPCM implicit water solvent. Save it as alanine_cpcm.xyz.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_93\"><figDesc>Now I'll create the alanine with 12 explicit water molecules plus CPCM water:Tool used:Tool Name: geometry_generation Arguments: message2agent: Please create the geometry for alanine molecule with 12 explicit water molecules surrounding it. Save it as alanine_explicit.xyz.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_95\"><figDesc>Now I'll create a detailed analysis report comparing the IR spectra across the three solvation models: Tool used: Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments: code: omitted for brevity... computational_chemist Action:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_98\"><figDesc>Tool Name: repl_tool Arguments:code: omitted for brevity...</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_99\"><figDesc>The ORCA input files for all 5 conformers have been generated and saved in their respective folders. Each input file uses the following core specifications:• Calculation: Geometry optimization and frequency (OPT FREQ) • Functional: PBE0 • Basis set: def2-SVP for all atoms, def2-ECP for Ce (via %basis block) • Dispersion: D4 • RIJCOSX approximation • DEFGRID2 integration grid • SCF autotraH: OFF, TightSCF, max iterations 500 • Geometry optimization: max iterations 500 matic_1_nunpairedes_0_charge_0_xtb.xyz All input files specify 16 cores and each depends on a corresponding .xyz file located in the same directory as the input file. Task complete. run_orca Action:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_100\"><figDesc>The content of cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ.inp specifies the following regarding cores and dependencies:• Number of cores: 16 (set by %pal nprocs 16 end) • Memory per core: 4000 MB (set by %maxcore 4000) • Dependencies and settings:Action:All four input files were checked for core count and dependencies. Here are the findings: 1. tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ/tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ.inp 2. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ/tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ.inp 3. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_-FREQ.inp 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_-FREQ.inp For all files: -Core count: 16 (set by %pal nprocs 16) -Memory: %maxcore 4000 -Method: PBE0/def2-SVP with D4 dispersion, RIJCOSX, DEFGRID2, TightSCF -ECP: def2-ECP for lanthanoids -SCF: AutotraH false, MaxIter 500, TightSCF true -Geometry: MaxIter 500, coordsys redundant, cartfallback true, ReducePrint true -Output: Print[ P_Basis ] 2, Print[ P_MOs ] 1, Print[P_hirshfeld] 1 -Each uses the appropriate xyzfile for the structure All input files are consistent with the required settings for core count and dependencies.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_102\"><figDesc>all five ORCA jobs in parallel using 16 cores for each job as specified. Each job received a unique SLURM job ID:1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ -Job ID: 230608 2. tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ -Job ID: 230609 3. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ -Job ID: 230610 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ -Job ID: 230611 5. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ -Job ID: 230612 Unfortunately, all five jobs failed with the same error: [file orca\\_tools/Tool-Scanner/qcscan1.cpp, line 106]: Unknown identifier in SCF block line 13: Last token: TIGHTSCF. The error indicates there is a syntax issue in the SCF block of all input files. Specifically, the parameter \"TightSCF\" in the SCF block is not being recognized by this version of ORCA. This parameter might be incorrectly formatted or not supported in the installed ORCA version.endThe ConvCriteria parameter is not present in any of these SCF blocks. Task complete.submit_slurm_job Action:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_103\"><figDesc>successfully submitted all five ORCA jobs in parallel with the corrected input files. All jobs have completed successfully with \"ORCA TERMINATED NORMALLY\" status. Here's a summary of the jobs: 1. cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ -Job ID: 230618 -Status: Completed normally 2. tri_tri_mer_capped_OPT_FREQ -Job ID: 230619 -Status: Completed normally 3. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_OPT_FREQ -Job ID: 230620 -Status: Completed normally 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_OPT_FREQ -Job ID: 230621 -Status: Completed normally 5. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_OPT_FREQ -Job ID: 230622 -Status: Completed normally The corrections made to the input files by removing the problematic SCF convergence criteria parameters resolved the previous errors. Each job was properly allocated 16 cores as specified. Each job directory now contains the completed calculation outputs, including: -Main output file (.out) -Final optimized structure (.xyz) -Trajectory file (_trj.xyz) -SLURM log files All five ORCA optimization and frequency calculations for the Ce(III) complexes are now successfully completed.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_104\"><figDesc>successfully submitted the ORCA job using the specified input file. The job has been completed with \"ORCA TERMINATED NORMALLY\" status. Here's a summary: Job Details: -Folder: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ -Input file: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_-removed2.inp -Output file: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.out -SLURM Job ID: 230634 -Cores used: 16 -Status: Completed normally The calculation used PBE0/def2-SVP with D4 dispersion, RIJCOSX approximation, and def2-ECP for the lanthanoid metal center as specified in the input file. The job completed successfully, and the output files have been generated in the cn9_YICLED_-OPT_FREQ directory, including: -cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.out (main output file) -cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.xyz (optimized structure) -cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_-removed2_trj.xyz (trajectory file) -cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2_atom58.out (additional output file) run_orca Action: The ORCA job has successfully completed! Here's a summary of what was done: 1. Job Details: • Input file: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.inp • Folder: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ • Resources: 16 cores (as specified in the input file) • SLURM Job ID: 230634 2. Results: • Status: Completed successfully (\"ORCA TERMINATED NORMALLY\") • Output files generated:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_106\"><figDesc>: cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ/cn9_YICLED_OPT_FREQ_removed2.out query_keys:['convergence_information']    </figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_110\"><figDesc>perform single point calculations for all five Ce complex conformers using their optimized geometries with the specified settings. Let me proceed step by step. First, I'll create job folders for each conformer with the \"_SP\" suffix and copy the optimized geometry files:Tool used:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_111\"><figDesc>custom ORCA %basis block for a Ce(III) complex. -All atoms use def2-SVPD basis set. -Ce uses def2-ECP effective core potential. Optimized %basis block: %basis Basis \"def2-SVPD\" ECP \"def2-ECP\" end This block ensures all atoms use def2-SVPD, and Ce uses the def2-ECP as required. No unnecessary auxiliary or decontraction options were included.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_113\"><figDesc>submit the five ORCA single-point jobs in parallel. First, let me check the input files to verify core counts and dependencies:Tool used:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_114\"><figDesc>The content of cn9_YICLED_SP/cn9_YICLED_SP.inp specifies the following regarding cores and dependencies:• Number of cores: 16 (set by %pal nprocs 16 end) • Memory per core: 4000 MB (set by %maxcore 4000) • No explicit external dependencies are specified in the input file. The calculation uses the wB97M-V functional, def2-SVPD basis set, VV10 dispersion, and def2-ECP for lanthanoids, all of which are standard ORCA features. Summary: -Cores: 16 -Memory per core: 4000 MB -No external dependencies specified; all methods and basis sets are standard within ORCA.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_116\"><figDesc>Based on the verification, all five input files are properly configured with 16 cores and require wB97M-V/def2-SVPD calculations. I'll now submit all five single-point jobs in parallel:Tool used:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_117\"><figDesc>submitted all five ORCA single-point jobs in parallel. Each job was allocated 16 cores as requested. However, all five jobs have completed with errors: All jobs failed with the same error: INPUT ERROR UNRECOGNIZED OR DUPLICATED KEYWORD(S) IN SIMPLE INPUT LINE VV10 The error message indicates that the \"VV10\" keyword in the input files is not recognized by the installed version of ORCA. This suggests that the version of ORCA being used might not support the VV10 non-local correlation functional or that the keyword syntax is not correctly formatted for this version. Job summary: 1. cn9_YICLED_SP -Job ID: 230635 -Failed with VV10 keyword error 2. tri_tri_-mer_capped_SP -Job ID: 230636 -Failed with VV10 keyword error 3. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_-SP -Job ID: 230637 -Failed with VV10 keyword error 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP -Job ID: 230638 -Failed with VV10 keyword error 5. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP -Job ID: 230639 -Failed with VV10 keyword error To successfully run these calculations, the input files would need to be modified to either: 1. Remove the VV10 dispersion keyword if it's not needed 2. Replace it with a supported dispersion correction method 3. Use the proper syntax for VV10 in this specific ORCA version Would you like me to modify the input files to use a different dispersion correction that's compatible with this ORCA version? run_orca Action: I need to fix the input files by modifying or removing the VV10 keyword. Let me debug the input files: Tool used: Tool Name: input_file_service Arguments: message2agent: The ERROR MESSAGE IS \"UNRECOGNIZED OR DUPLICATED KEYWORD(S) IN SIMPLE INPUT LINE VV10\". Please debug the following ORCA input files by removing the \"VV10\" keyword from the simple input line (this is likely in the first few lines of the file where method/basis set specifications are defined): 1. cn9_YICLED_SP/cn9_YICLED_SP.inp 2. tri_-tri_mer_capped_SP/tri_tri_mer_capped_SP.inp 3. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP/tricapped_-trigonal_prismatic_SP.inp 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP/capped_square_antiprismatic_-0_SP.inp 5. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP.inp Simply remove the VV10 keyword while keeping all other settings intact.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_118\"><figDesc>omitted for brevity...</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_119\"><figDesc>\" keyword has been removed from the simple input line in the following ORCA input files to resolve the \"UNRECOGNIZED OR DUPLICATED KEYWORD(S) IN SIMPLE INPUT LINE VV10\" error:1. cn9_YICLED_SP/cn9_YICLED_SP.inp 2. tri_tri_mer_capped_SP/tri_tri_mer_capped_SP.inp 3. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP/tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP.inp 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP/capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP.inp 5. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP/capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP.inp All other computational parameters and settings remain unchanged. Each file was overwritten in place and is dependent on its respective xyz file in the same directory. The core specifications for each input Action:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_120\"><figDesc>Now that I've confirmed the VV10 keyword has been removed from all input files, I'll submit the five single-point jobs in parallel:Tool used:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_121\"><figDesc>successfully submitted all five ORCA single-point jobs in parallel with the corrected input files. All jobs have completed successfully with \"ORCA TERMINATED NORMALLY\" status. Here's a summary: 1. cn9_YICLED_SP -Job ID: 230640 -Status: Completed normally 2. tri_tri_mer_capped_SP -Job ID: 230641 -Status: Completed normally 3. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP -Job ID: 230642 -Status: Completed normally 4. capped_square_antiprismatic_0_SP -Job ID: 230643 -Status: Completed normally 5. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP -Job ID: 230644 -Status: Completed normally The input files were successfully corrected by removing the VV10 keyword that was causing errors in the previous run. Each job was allocated 16 cores as specified, and all calculations used the wB97M-V functional with def2-SVPD basis set. Each job directory now contains the completed calculation outputs, including: -Main output file (.out) -Atom information file (_atom58.out) -SLURM log files All five single-point energy calculations for the Ce(III) complexes have been completed successfully.</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_123\"><figDesc>The first submission of all five jobs failed with the error \"UNRECOGNIZED OR DUPLI-CATED KEYWORD(S) IN SIMPLE INPUT LINE VV10\" • This indicated that the installed ORCA version does not support the VV10 non-local correlation functional keyword 3. Debug and Fix:• I used the input file service to remove the VV10 keyword from all five input files • All other computational parameters remained unchanged, including the wB97M-V functional and def2-SVPD basis set 4. Successful Completion:• The resubmitted jobs completed successfully with \"ORCA TERMINATED NORMALLY\" status • Each job was allocated 16 cores as specified • All output files were generated correctly The following jobs were completed: 1. cn9_YICLED_SP (Job ID: 230640) 2. tri_tri_mer_capped_-SP (Job ID: 230641) 3. tricapped_trigonal_prismatic_SP (Job ID: 230642) 4. capped_square_-antiprismatic_0_SP (Job ID: 230643) 5. capped_square_antiprismatic_1_SP (Job ID: 230644) Each job folder now contains the completed calculation outputs including the main output file (.out) and atom information file (_atom58.out).</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_124\"><figDesc>Now I'll check if all the calculations completed successfully:Tool used:</figDesc></figure>\n<figure xmlns=\"http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0\" xml:id=\"fig_126\"><figDesc>Now I'll extract the energies from the output files for all conformers to prepare a final report:Tool used:</figDesc></figure>\n\t\t\t</div>\n\t\t\t<div type=\"references\">\n\n\t\t\t\t<listBibl>\n\n<biblStruct status=\"extracted\" xml:id=\"b0\">\n\t<monogr>\n\t\t<title level=\"m\" type=\"main\">Introduction to Computational Chemistry</title>\n\t\t<author>\n\t\t\t<persName><forename type=\"first\">Frank</forename><su